(window.__PQ=window.__PQ||{})["10-aws"]={"slug":"10-aws","title":"Amazon Web Services","accent":"#f59e0b","sections":[{"id":"s01-accounts-organizations","title":"Accounts, Organizations and Control Tower","c":[{"id":"s01-accounts-organizations-c1","t":"The AWS account as a boundary","p":"An account is a container for resources, with its own users, quotas, bill and blast radius. Nothing is shared with another account unless you explicitly allow it.","k":[["Why is an account a stronger boundary than a VPC or a tag?","IAM, quotas, billing and resources are all scoped to it, so a mistake or compromise cannot cross it without an explicit cross-account trust."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-accounts-organizations-c2","t":"AWS Organizations and OUs","p":"AWS Organizations joins many accounts under one management account. Accounts are arranged in a tree of organizational units (OUs) under a root, and policies attached to a node apply to everything below it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-accounts-organizations-c3","t":"Multi-account strategy","p":"A multi-account strategy decides which accounts exist and why. The usual split is by function (security, network, shared tooling) and then by workload and environment.","k":[["Which account should hold organization-wide audit logs?","A dedicated log archive account with restricted write access, not the management account and not the workload accounts that produce the logs."]],"o":[["Per application and environment","prod needs strict isolation and clear cost","you have a handful of tiny services with one owner"],["Per environment shared by apps","small team, few apps","teams must not see each other's data"],["Shared sandbox accounts","learning with automatic cleanup","anything holding real data"]]},{"id":"s01-accounts-organizations-c4","t":"Service control policies","p":"An SCP is a ceiling on what any principal in an account can do. It never grants access; it only removes the possibility of it.","k":[["Can an SCP allow an action that IAM does not?","No. An SCP only limits. The identity or resource policy still has to grant the permission."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-accounts-organizations-c5","t":"Other organization policies and RCPs","p":"SCPs control what your principals can do. Resource control policies (RCPs) control what can be done to your resources, whoever the caller is. Tag, backup and declarative policies standardise configuration.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-accounts-organizations-c6","t":"Control Tower and landing zones","p":"AWS Control Tower builds and maintains a landing zone: a pre-configured multi-account environment with logging, guardrails and an account vending machine.","k":[["What is the difference between a preventive and a detective control?","Preventive blocks the action up front (SCP or RCP). Detective only reports non-compliance after the fact (an AWS Config rule)."]],"o":[["Control Tower","you want a supported baseline fast","you need an unusual layout it cannot model"],["Organizations plus Terraform","you want full control and have platform engineers","you have no team to maintain it"],["Custom plus Control Tower controls","you adopt controls but vend accounts yourself","you want zero custom code"]]},{"id":"s01-accounts-organizations-c7","t":"Account lifecycle, billing and quotas","p":"Accounts are created, tagged, budgeted, and eventually closed. Each has quotas that you may need to raise.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s02-iam-principals-policies","title":"IAM principals and policies","c":[{"id":"s02-iam-principals-policies-c1","t":"Principals, identities and the root user","p":"A principal is whoever makes a request. In IAM it is a user, a role, a federated identity, an AWS service, or an anonymous caller. The root user is the account owner with unrestricted power.","k":[["Can a group be the principal in a role trust policy?","No. Groups are only containers for attaching permissions to users."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-iam-principals-policies-c2","t":"Anatomy of a policy","p":"A policy is a JSON document listing statements. Each statement says Allow or Deny, for which actions, on which resources, and optionally under which conditions.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-iam-principals-policies-c3","t":"Identity-based, resource-based and trust policies","p":"Permissions can be attached to the caller (identity-based), to the thing being called (resource-based), or to a role to say who may assume it (trust policy).","k":[["Where do you say who can assume a role?","In the role's trust policy, which is a resource-based policy attached to the role."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-iam-principals-policies-c4","t":"Managed, inline and customer policies","p":"A managed policy is a standalone policy attached in many places. An inline policy is embedded in one identity. AWS managed policies are written by AWS; customer managed ones are yours.","k":[],"o":[["Customer managed","reuse and version control","a one-off tightly bound to one role"],["Inline","a permission that must never be reused","many roles need the same grant"],["AWS managed","quick read-only exploration","production least privilege"]]},{"id":"s02-iam-principals-policies-c5","t":"ARNs, wildcards and policy variables","p":"An ARN names a resource: arn:partition:service:region:account:resource. Wildcards and ? and variables like ${aws:username} let one policy cover many resources.","k":[["Why do S3 ARNs have no account or Region?","Bucket names are globally unique within a partition, so the ARN needs only the name."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-iam-principals-policies-c6","t":"Least privilege in practice","p":"Least privilege means granting only what is needed. In practice you start narrow, observe denials, and tighten from usage data.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-iam-principals-policies-c7","t":"Passwords, MFA and access keys","p":"Humans prove identity with a password plus MFA; programs with access keys or, better, temporary credentials.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s03-iam-evaluation-conditions","title":"IAM evaluation logic, conditions and boundaries","c":[{"id":"s03-iam-evaluation-conditions-c1","t":"The evaluation order","p":"For each request AWS gathers every applicable policy and decides with a simple rule: explicit deny wins, otherwise something must allow, otherwise the default is deny.","k":[["If an SCP allows an action and the identity policy has no matching Allow, what happens?","Implicit deny. An SCP never grants permissions."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-iam-evaluation-conditions-c2","t":"Same-account versus cross-account","p":"Inside one account, an identity policy or a resource policy can alone grant access. Across accounts both the caller's account and the resource's account must allow.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-iam-evaluation-conditions-c3","t":"Condition operators and context keys","p":"A Condition block compares context keys (facts about the request) with values using operators. All keys in a block must match (AND); multiple values for one key are OR.","k":[["How are two keys in one Condition block combined?","With AND: every key must match for the statement to apply."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-iam-evaluation-conditions-c4","t":"Tag-based access control","p":"ABAC (attribute-based access control) compares tags on the principal with tags on the resource, so one policy scales to many teams.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-iam-evaluation-conditions-c5","t":"Permission boundaries","p":"A permission boundary is a managed policy set on a user or role as a maximum. The identity gets only what its own policies allow AND the boundary allows.","k":[["Does a permission boundary give permissions?","No. It only limits what the identity policies can grant."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-iam-evaluation-conditions-c6","t":"Session policies and privilege escalation paths","p":"A session policy is passed when assuming a role and further restricts that one session. Escalation paths are combinations of permissions that let an identity gain more power.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s04-roles-sts-identity-center","title":"Roles, STS and Identity Center","c":[{"id":"s04-roles-sts-identity-center-c1","t":"IAM roles and trust","p":"A role is an identity with permissions but no password or keys. Anyone allowed by its trust policy can ask STS for temporary credentials for it.","k":[["What two kinds of policy define a role?","A trust policy (who can assume it) and permission policies (what the session may do)."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-roles-sts-identity-center-c2","t":"STS and temporary credentials","p":"AWS STS issues temporary credentials. They consist of an access key ID, a secret and a session token, and they expire.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-roles-sts-identity-center-c3","t":"Cross-account access and the confused deputy","p":"To reach resources in another account you assume a role there. When a third party does it on your behalf, add an external ID so they cannot be tricked into acting for the wrong customer.","k":[["Which condition protects against the confused deputy for a third party?","sts:ExternalId in the role trust policy."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-roles-sts-identity-center-c4","t":"Instance profiles and service roles","p":"An instance profile is the wrapper that attaches a role to an EC2 instance. Other services (Lambda, ECS tasks, CodeBuild) have service roles with their own trust.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-roles-sts-identity-center-c5","t":"Federation with OIDC for CI systems","p":"Instead of storing AWS keys in CI, the CI system presents a signed OIDC token and AWS exchanges it for role credentials.","k":[["Which claim restricts which repo may assume the role?","The sub claim, matched in the trust policy condition."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-roles-sts-identity-center-c6","t":"IAM Identity Center","p":"IAM Identity Center is single sign-on for your AWS accounts. People log in once with your company identity provider and pick an account and role.","k":[],"o":[["Identity Center","any multi-account organization","a single tiny account with one admin"],["IAM users","break-glass emergency only","everyday human access"],["SAML federation per account","legacy IdP integrations","you can use Identity Center instead"]]}]},{"id":"s05-workload-identity-analyzer","title":"Workload identity for Kubernetes and Access Analyzer","c":[{"id":"s05-workload-identity-analyzer-c1","t":"Why pods need their own identity","p":"If a pod uses the node's instance role, every pod on that node gets the same permissions. Pod-level identity gives each service account only the role it needs.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-workload-identity-analyzer-c2","t":"IRSA mechanics","p":"IAM Roles for Service Accounts uses the cluster's OIDC issuer. Kubernetes projects a signed token into the pod, and the AWS SDK exchanges it for role credentials.","k":[["What does the webhook inject into the pod for IRSA?","AWS_ROLE_ARN, AWS_WEB_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE and a projected service account token volume."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-workload-identity-analyzer-c3","t":"EKS Pod Identity","p":"EKS Pod Identity associates an IAM role with a service account through the EKS API. An agent on each node serves credentials to the pods.","k":[],"o":[["Pod Identity","new EKS clusters and many clusters sharing roles","non-EKS clusters or compute types it does not support"],["IRSA","clusters outside EKS, or Fargate where supported only via IRSA","you want to avoid per-cluster trust edits"],["Node role","never for workloads","any workload with real permissions"]]},{"id":"s05-workload-identity-analyzer-c4","t":"Scoping and ABAC for pods","p":"Role design for pods follows the same rules as everywhere, with the extra goal of one role per workload, and policies that use session tags.","k":[["Why set IMDS hop limit to 1 on nodes?","A container adds a network hop, so pods cannot reach the metadata service and borrow the node role."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-workload-identity-analyzer-c5","t":"IAM Access Analyzer","p":"IAM Access Analyzer finds resources shared outside your account or organization, unused access, and validates policies.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-workload-identity-analyzer-c6","t":"Auditing IAM with CloudTrail","p":"CloudTrail records API calls. It is the evidence for who assumed which role, which action was denied, and what a role really uses.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s06-vpc-cidr-subnets","title":"VPC fundamentals, CIDR planning and subnets","c":[{"id":"s06-vpc-cidr-subnets-c1","t":"What a VPC is","p":"A VPC is a virtual network that you define with an IP range. Resources such as EC2 instances, load balancers, databases and EKS nodes get private IP addresses from it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-vpc-cidr-subnets-c2","t":"CIDR planning","p":"CIDR notation 10.20.0.0/16 means the first 16 bits are fixed and the remaining 16 are yours: 65,536 addresses. A smaller number after the slash means a bigger network.","k":[["How many addresses can you use in a /24 subnet?","251, because AWS reserves five of the 256."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-vpc-cidr-subnets-c3","t":"Subnets and Availability Zones","p":"A subnet is a slice of the VPC range placed in one AZ. Whether it is \"public\" or \"private\" depends only on its route table.","k":[["What makes a subnet public?","A route in its route table to an Internet Gateway (plus a public or Elastic IP on the resource)."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-vpc-cidr-subnets-c4","t":"Route tables","p":"A route table is a list of destination ranges and where to send them. The most specific match (longest prefix) wins.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-vpc-cidr-subnets-c5","t":"Internet Gateway and public addressing","p":"An Internet Gateway (IGW) connects a VPC to the public internet. It performs one-to-one NAT between a private address and the instance's public or Elastic IP.","k":[["Why can an instance with a public IP still be unreachable?","The route, security group or NACL may not allow it; all of them must permit the traffic."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-vpc-cidr-subnets-c6","t":"NAT gateways","p":"A NAT gateway lets instances in private subnets start connections to the internet while blocking inbound connections from it.","k":[],"o":[["One NAT per AZ","production","tiny dev environments"],["Single NAT","dev and cost-sensitive tests","production with availability goals"],["VPC endpoints plus NAT","heavy traffic to S3, ECR and other AWS services","you only call third-party hosts"]]}]},{"id":"s07-vpc-endpoints-connectivity","title":"Endpoints, PrivateLink, peering and Transit Gateway","c":[{"id":"s07-vpc-endpoints-connectivity-c1","t":"Gateway endpoints","p":"A gateway endpoint adds a route in your route table that sends S3 or DynamoDB traffic over the AWS network, not through NAT or the internet.","k":[["Why is the S3 gateway endpoint a good first cost saver?","It is free and removes S3 traffic from the NAT gateway data processing charge."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-vpc-endpoints-connectivity-c2","t":"Interface endpoints and PrivateLink","p":"An interface endpoint puts an elastic network interface with a private IP in your subnet that fronts an AWS service or a partner service. It is powered by AWS PrivateLink.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-vpc-endpoints-connectivity-c3","t":"Publishing a service with PrivateLink","p":"A service provider puts a Network Load Balancer (or Gateway Load Balancer) in front of the service and creates an endpoint service. Consumers create an interface endpoint to it.","k":[["Can PrivateLink work between VPCs with the same CIDR?","Yes. Only the endpoint ENI address is used, not routing between the ranges."]],"o":[["PrivateLink","one service shared with many consumers or overlapping CIDRs","all-to-all network connectivity"],["Peering or TGW","broad network reachability","you need to hide the rest of the network"]]},{"id":"s07-vpc-endpoints-connectivity-c4","t":"VPC peering","p":"A peering connection links two VPCs so they route to each other using private IPs, in the same or different accounts and Regions.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-vpc-endpoints-connectivity-c5","t":"Transit Gateway","p":"A Transit Gateway (TGW) is a regional hub. VPCs, VPNs and Direct Connect attach to it, and route tables on the TGW decide who can talk to whom.","k":[["How does a TGW provide isolation between prod and dev?","Separate TGW route tables, with each attachment associated to one table and propagating only to the tables that should reach it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-vpc-endpoints-connectivity-c6","t":"VPN, Direct Connect and choosing connectivity","p":"Site-to-Site VPN gives encrypted tunnels over the internet. Direct Connect is a dedicated private link from your site to AWS.","k":[],"o":[["Peering","two VPCs, simple and cheap","many VPCs or on-premises transit"],["Transit Gateway","many VPCs, segmentation, hybrid","two VPCs that never grow"],["PrivateLink","publish one service","full network reachability needed"],["Site-to-Site VPN","fast start, backup path","large steady throughput needs"],["Direct Connect","steady, high volume, consistent latency","you need it this week"]]}]},{"id":"s08-security-groups-nacls-flowlogs","title":"Security groups, NACLs and flow logs","c":[{"id":"s08-security-groups-nacls-flowlogs-c1","t":"Security groups","p":"A security group is a stateful firewall attached to a network interface (an instance, load balancer, endpoint or database). It lists what is allowed in and out.","k":[["Why is a security group rule that references another group better than a CIDR?","It follows instances as they scale or change IPs and states the intent directly."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-security-groups-nacls-flowlogs-c2","t":"Network ACLs","p":"A network ACL (NACL) is a stateless firewall on the subnet boundary. Rules are numbered and evaluated in order, and they can allow or deny.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-security-groups-nacls-flowlogs-c3","t":"Debugging with Reachability Analyzer","p":"Reachability Analyzer examines your configuration, not live traffic, and tells you whether a path between two resources exists and which component blocks it.","k":[["Does Reachability Analyzer send real packets?","No. It analyses the configuration of the path."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-security-groups-nacls-flowlogs-c4","t":"VPC Flow Logs","p":"Flow logs record metadata about IP traffic: source, destination, ports, protocol, bytes and whether it was accepted or rejected. They do not contain payloads.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-security-groups-nacls-flowlogs-c5","t":"Network Firewall, Traffic Mirroring and egress control","p":"For deeper control than security groups, use AWS Network Firewall for stateful inspection and domain filtering, and Traffic Mirroring to copy packets for analysis.","k":[],"o":[["Security group only","simple internal services","you must restrict by domain name"],["Network Firewall","domain and protocol filtering centrally","a tiny dev account"],["Proxy (Squid) on EC2","existing proxy skills, per-user logging","you want fully managed"]]}]},{"id":"s09-route53-dns","title":"Route 53 and DNS in AWS","c":[{"id":"s09-route53-dns-c1","t":"Hosted zones and records","p":"A hosted zone holds the DNS records for a domain. A public zone answers anyone on the internet; a private zone answers only inside the VPCs you associate.","k":[["Why use an alias instead of a CNAME at the zone apex?","A CNAME cannot coexist with the other apex records (SOA, NS); an alias is answered as an A or AAAA record and can sit at the apex."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-route53-dns-c2","t":"Routing policies","p":"A routing policy decides which answer Route 53 gives when several records share a name.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-route53-dns-c3","t":"Health checks and failover","p":"Route 53 health checks probe an endpoint from several locations and mark it healthy or not. Records tied to a failing check are removed from answers.","k":[["Why keep a low TTL on failover records?","Resolvers cache answers for the TTL, so a long TTL delays clients moving to the secondary."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-route53-dns-c4","t":"Private hosted zones","p":"A private hosted zone serves DNS names only to the VPCs associated with it, such as db.internal.example.com.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-route53-dns-c5","t":"Route 53 Resolver","p":"The Route 53 Resolver is the DNS server every VPC already has at the VPC base address plus two (for example 10.20.0.2) and at 169.254.169.253. Resolver endpoints connect it with on-premises DNS.","k":[],"o":[["ExternalDNS with Route 53","Ingress and Service hostnames managed from the cluster","names must be created by a separate process"],["Manual records","a handful of stable names","frequent app changes"],["Private zone plus Resolver rules","hybrid internal naming","public-only services"]]}]},{"id":"s10-ipv6-vpc-design","title":"IPv6 and reference VPC designs","c":[{"id":"s10-ipv6-vpc-design-c1","t":"IPv6 in a VPC","p":"IPv6 addresses are 128 bits long and globally unique. AWS gives a VPC a /56 block, and each subnet gets a /64.","k":[["How do private subnets get IPv6 outbound access without inbound?","Route ::/0 to an egress-only internet gateway."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-ipv6-vpc-design-c2","t":"IPv6 and EKS","p":"An EKS cluster can be created in IPv6 mode, where pods receive IPv6 addresses and nodes keep IPv4 for the control plane path.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-ipv6-vpc-design-c3","t":"Reference three-tier VPC","p":"A common production layout uses three AZs, with public, private and isolated subnets in each, one NAT gateway per AZ and endpoints for AWS services.","k":[["Where should databases live in this design?","In isolated subnets with no route to an internet or NAT gateway."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-ipv6-vpc-design-c4","t":"Production VPC checklist","p":"A short list to review before an environment goes live.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s11-ec2-instances-nitro","title":"EC2 instance families and the Nitro system","c":[{"id":"s11-ec2-instances-nitro-c1","t":"Reading an instance type","p":"A name like m7g.2xlarge encodes the family (m), generation (7), extra attributes (g) and size (2xlarge).","k":[["What does the g in m7g mean?","The instance uses an AWS Graviton (Arm) processor."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-ec2-instances-nitro-c2","t":"Choosing a family","p":"Match the bottleneck of your workload to the family: CPU, memory, storage speed or GPU.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-ec2-instances-nitro-c3","t":"The Nitro system","p":"Nitro is the AWS hypervisor and hardware platform. Dedicated Nitro cards handle networking, storage and management, leaving almost all of the host CPU to your instance.","k":[["What does Nitro give besides speed?","Hardware-enforced isolation, no operator access to instance memory, bare metal options and Enclaves."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-ec2-instances-nitro-c4","t":"Graviton and processor choices","p":"Graviton instances use Arm processors designed by AWS. They usually offer better price per performance, but need Arm-compatible binaries and images.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-ec2-instances-nitro-c5","t":"CPU, vCPU and burstable credits","p":"A vCPU is a hardware thread (usually half a core on x86 with hyperthreading; a full core on Graviton). Burstable t instances earn credits when idle and spend them when busy.","k":[["Why can a t3 instance suddenly become slow?","Its CPU credit balance reached zero, so it is held at baseline performance."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-ec2-instances-nitro-c6","t":"Instance lifecycle and metadata","p":"An instance moves through pending, running, stopping, stopped, and terminated states. The instance metadata service describes it from the inside.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s12-ebs-storage-network","title":"EBS, instance store and instance networking","c":[{"id":"s12-ebs-storage-network-c1","t":"EBS volume types","p":"Amazon EBS is network-attached block storage that persists independently of the instance. You pick a volume type by the performance profile you need.","k":[["How does gp3 differ from gp2 for performance?","gp3 has a fixed baseline and lets you provision IOPS and throughput independently of size; gp2 ties IOPS to size."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-ebs-storage-network-c2","t":"Instance limits on storage throughput","p":"Each instance type has its own maximum EBS bandwidth and IOPS. A fast volume on a small instance is capped by the instance.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-ebs-storage-network-c3","t":"Snapshots, encryption and fast restore","p":"A snapshot is a point-in-time copy of a volume stored in S3-managed storage. Only changed blocks are saved after the first one.","k":[["Why use EBS encryption by default?","Any new volume is encrypted automatically, so nobody can forget to enable it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-ebs-storage-network-c4","t":"Instance store","p":"Instance store is local NVMe or SSD storage physically attached to the host. It is very fast and included in the instance price, but temporary.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-ebs-storage-network-c5","t":"ENIs, ENA and EFA","p":"An elastic network interface (ENI) is a virtual network card with private IPs, a MAC and security groups. ENA is the high-performance driver on Nitro. EFA adds OS-bypass networking for HPC and ML.","k":[["What does the ENA exceeded counter tell you?","The instance hit a network allowance (bandwidth, packets per second, connection tracking), so packets were dropped or queued."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s13-placement-spot-purchasing","title":"Placement groups, Spot and purchasing options","c":[{"id":"s13-placement-spot-purchasing-c1","t":"Placement groups","p":"A placement group influences how EC2 places instances on the underlying hardware.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-placement-spot-purchasing-c2","t":"On-Demand and capacity reservations","p":"On-Demand is pay-per-second with no commitment. Capacity Reservations guarantee that capacity exists in an AZ, whether or not you run instances.","k":[["What does a Capacity Reservation guarantee that a Savings Plan does not?","That the capacity is available in a given AZ; a Savings Plan is only a billing discount."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-placement-spot-purchasing-c3","t":"Spot Instances","p":"Spot uses spare EC2 capacity at a large discount, with the catch that AWS can take it back with a two-minute warning.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-placement-spot-purchasing-c4","t":"Savings Plans and Reserved Instances","p":"Savings Plans are a commitment to spend a fixed amount per hour for one or three years in return for a lower rate. Reserved Instances commit to specific instance attributes.","k":[["Should you buy a Savings Plan for your peak hourly spend?","No. Commit to the steady minimum; cover peaks with On-Demand or Spot."]],"o":[["On-Demand","unknown or short-lived load","steady 24/7 baseline"],["Spot","stateless, interruptible work","single stateful instance"],["Compute Savings Plan","steady baseline with changing instance types","usage you may stop within the term"],["EC2 Instance Savings Plan","long-lived known family","you plan to move to another family soon"],["Capacity Reservation","must-have capacity in an AZ","flexible workloads that can retry elsewhere"]]},{"id":"s13-placement-spot-purchasing-c5","t":"Tenancy, Dedicated Hosts and licences","p":"By default instances share physical hosts with other customers (shared tenancy). Dedicated options give you hardware not shared.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s14-user-data-ssm","title":"User data, metadata and Systems Manager","c":[{"id":"s14-user-data-ssm-c1","t":"User data and cloud-init","p":"User data is a script or cloud-config you pass at launch. The instance runs it once at first boot through cloud-init.","k":[["Where can you read what user data ran?","In /var/log/cloud-init-output.log and `cloud-init status --long`."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-user-data-ssm-c2","t":"Launch templates","p":"A launch template is a versioned recipe for an instance: AMI, type, key, roles, network, storage, tags and user data.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-user-data-ssm-c3","t":"SSM Session Manager","p":"Session Manager gives you a shell on an instance through the SSM agent, with no inbound ports, bastion hosts or SSH keys.","k":[["What must an instance have to be reachable with Session Manager?","The SSM agent, an instance profile permitting SSM, and outbound HTTPS to the SSM endpoints."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-user-data-ssm-c4","t":"Run Command, State Manager and Patch Manager","p":"Systems Manager can run commands on many instances, keep them in a desired state, and patch them on a schedule.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-user-data-ssm-c5","t":"Parameter Store and Secrets Manager","p":"Parameter Store holds configuration values and optionally encrypted secrets. Secrets Manager adds automatic rotation for credentials.","k":[],"o":[["Parameter Store","configuration and simple secrets","automatic rotation required"],["Secrets Manager","database credentials with rotation","plain config values"],["Kubernetes Secret","in-cluster consumption","long-term source of truth without encryption at rest"]]}]},{"id":"s15-amis-image-pipelines","title":"AMIs and image pipelines","c":[{"id":"s15-amis-image-pipelines-c1","t":"What an AMI is","p":"An Amazon Machine Image bundles a root volume snapshot, launch permissions and a block device mapping. You launch instances from it in the same Region.","k":[["Can you launch an AMI in another Region directly?","No. Copy it to that Region first."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-amis-image-pipelines-c2","t":"Packer","p":"Packer builds machine images from code. It launches a temporary instance, runs provisioners, snapshots it as an AMI and cleans up.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-amis-image-pipelines-c3","t":"EC2 Image Builder","p":"EC2 Image Builder is the managed AWS pipeline for images: recipes, components, tests, distribution and scheduled rebuilds.","k":[["How does a launch template learn about a new AMI?","From an SSM parameter updated by the pipeline, resolved with resolve:ssm in the template."]],"o":[["EC2 Image Builder","managed, scheduled, AWS-native","multi-cloud images from one tool"],["Packer","one tool for many clouds and local testing","you want no build code to maintain"],["Bottlerocket","container hosts, immutable","you need general-purpose package management"]]},{"id":"s15-amis-image-pipelines-c4","t":"EKS-optimized AMIs and Bottlerocket","p":"EKS provides AMIs with kubelet and container runtime preinstalled. Bottlerocket is a minimal, immutable, container-focused OS.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-amis-image-pipelines-c5","t":"AMI lifecycle and deprecation","p":"Old AMIs accumulate snapshots, cost money, and may contain vulnerabilities. A lifecycle policy removes them.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s16-elb-overview-alb","title":"Elastic Load Balancing and the Application Load Balancer","c":[{"id":"s16-elb-overview-alb-c1","t":"The three load balancer types","p":"Elastic Load Balancing offers three main types, each working at a different network layer: ALB for HTTP, NLB for TCP, UDP and TLS, GWLB for network appliances.","k":[["Which load balancer keeps the client source IP and gives you a static IP?","The NLB."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-elb-overview-alb-c2","t":"ALB architecture","p":"An ALB is a set of managed nodes spread across the subnets you choose. DNS resolves its name to the IP addresses of those nodes, which scale up and down with load.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-elb-overview-alb-c3","t":"Listeners and rules","p":"A listener checks for connections on a port and protocol. Its rules pick an action based on conditions, evaluated in priority order.","k":[["Which rule wins when two match?","The one with the lower priority number."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-elb-overview-alb-c4","t":"Target groups","p":"A target group is the set of backends a rule forwards to, with its own protocol, port, health check and attributes.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-elb-overview-alb-c5","t":"Security groups and client IP","p":"The load balancer and its targets each have security groups. The target should accept traffic only from the load balancer's group.","k":[["How does an app behind an ALB learn the real client IP?","From the X-Forwarded-For header that the ALB adds."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-elb-overview-alb-c6","t":"Authentication and special actions","p":"An ALB can authenticate users with OIDC or Cognito before forwarding, and can answer requests itself.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s17-nlb-gwlb","title":"Network Load Balancer and Gateway Load Balancer","c":[{"id":"s17-nlb-gwlb-c1","t":"NLB basics","p":"The Network Load Balancer forwards TCP, UDP and TLS connections at layer 4 with very low latency. It does not look inside HTTP.","k":[["Why use an NLB when firewalls on the partner side need fixed IPs?","NLB gives one static IP per AZ, and Elastic IPs for internet-facing ones, so allow-lists stay stable."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-nlb-gwlb-c2","t":"NLB TLS, health checks and draining","p":"An NLB can terminate TLS, run TCP or HTTP health checks, and hold existing connections during deregistration.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-nlb-gwlb-c3","t":"NLB with PrivateLink and ALB-type targets","p":"An NLB is the entry point for PrivateLink endpoint services, and an NLB can forward to an ALB to combine static IPs with layer 7 routing.","k":[],"o":[["ALB only","HTTP routing, WAF and no IP allow-list needed","clients need fixed IPs"],["NLB then ALB","fixed IPs plus path routing","the extra hop and cost are unacceptable"],["NLB only","pure TCP or UDP","you need header based routing"]]},{"id":"s17-nlb-gwlb-c4","t":"Gateway Load Balancer","p":"GWLB puts a fleet of virtual appliances (firewalls, intrusion prevention) transparently in the path of traffic, and scales them as one.","k":[["Which port and protocol does GWLB use to talk to appliances?","GENEVE over UDP 6081."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-nlb-gwlb-c5","t":"Choosing and costing","p":"Each type has a different pricing metric and failure behaviour, so decide with both technical and cost data.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s18-tls-waf-cloudfront","title":"TLS certificates, WAF and CloudFront","c":[{"id":"s18-tls-waf-cloudfront-c1","t":"AWS Certificate Manager","p":"ACM issues and renews public TLS certificates for free when used with integrated services (ALB, NLB, CloudFront, API Gateway).","k":[["In which Region must a CloudFront certificate live?","us-east-1."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-tls-waf-cloudfront-c2","t":"TLS policies and end-to-end encryption","p":"A security policy on a listener sets the TLS versions and ciphers it accepts. For sensitive data you can also encrypt between the load balancer and the targets.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-tls-waf-cloudfront-c3","t":"AWS WAF","p":"AWS WAF inspects HTTP requests on an ALB, CloudFront, API Gateway or AppSync and allows, blocks, counts or challenges them.","k":[["Why start with count mode?","It shows what would be blocked without affecting real users, so you can tune false positives."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-tls-waf-cloudfront-c4","t":"CloudFront basics","p":"CloudFront is a global CDN. Edge locations cache content close to users and speed up dynamic traffic via the AWS backbone.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-tls-waf-cloudfront-c5","t":"Securing the origin and combining edge services","p":"If CloudFront and WAF protect you, attackers must not be able to bypass them by calling the origin directly.","k":[["How do you make sure traffic cannot skip CloudFront?","Allow only the CloudFront origin-facing prefix list (and a secret header) on the origin."]],"o":[["CloudFront plus ALB","global users and cacheable content","purely internal services"],["ALB with WAF only","one Region, mostly dynamic","heavy static assets"],["API Gateway","managed API features such as keys and throttling","you already have an ALB doing routing"]]}]},{"id":"s19-load-balancing-kubernetes","title":"Load balancing for Kubernetes and operations","c":[{"id":"s19-load-balancing-kubernetes-c1","t":"AWS Load Balancer Controller","p":"The AWS Load Balancer Controller runs in your cluster, watches Ingress and Service objects and creates ALBs, NLBs, listeners and target groups.","k":[["How can many Ingresses share one ALB?","Give them the same alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/group.name annotation."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-load-balancing-kubernetes-c2","t":"Instance mode versus IP mode","p":"The target group can register nodes (instance mode, via NodePort) or pod IPs directly (IP mode).","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-load-balancing-kubernetes-c3","t":"Zero-downtime deployments","p":"Pods must leave the load balancer before they stop, and join it only when ready. Several timers make this work.","k":[["Why a preStop sleep?","The load balancer needs seconds to stop sending new requests, so the pod must keep serving until deregistration takes effect."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-load-balancing-kubernetes-c4","t":"Health checks in depth","p":"A target is healthy when it passes a configured number of consecutive probes, unhealthy after a number of failures. Settings control how quickly the load balancer reacts.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-load-balancing-kubernetes-c5","t":"Monitoring and troubleshooting","p":"Metrics, access logs and a few common error codes quickly show whether a problem is the client, the load balancer or the target.","k":[["What is the first thing to check for a 503 from an ALB?","Whether the target group has any healthy targets."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s20-asg-fundamentals","title":"Auto Scaling groups and launch templates","c":[{"id":"s20-asg-fundamentals-c1","t":"What an ASG does","p":"An ASG is a controller. You say minimum, desired and maximum instance counts and which template to use; it launches and terminates instances to match.","k":[["Which values bound what a scaling policy can do?","MinSize and MaxSize; policies only change DesiredCapacity inside them."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-asg-fundamentals-c2","t":"Launch templates in an ASG","p":"The ASG reads the launch template at each launch. Which version it uses is a setting on the group.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-asg-fundamentals-c3","t":"Health checks and grace period","p":"An ASG decides an instance is unhealthy from EC2 status checks, and optionally from the load balancer's view.","k":[["What problem does the grace period solve?","It stops the ASG from replacing instances that are still booting and not yet able to pass health checks."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-asg-fundamentals-c4","t":"Availability Zones and rebalancing","p":"An ASG tries to keep an equal number of instances per AZ. If an AZ fails or lacks capacity, it launches elsewhere.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-asg-fundamentals-c5","t":"Mixed instances and attribute-based selection","p":"A single instance type can run out of capacity or Spot supply. A mixed instances policy lets one ASG use several types and purchase options.","k":[["What does an On-Demand base capacity of 2 mean?","The first two instances are always On-Demand; the rest follow the Spot/On-Demand percentage."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s21-scaling-policies","title":"Scaling policies and scheduled scaling","c":[{"id":"s21-scaling-policies-c1","t":"Target tracking","p":"You choose a metric and a target value, such as 50 percent average CPU. AWS creates the alarms and adjusts capacity to hold the metric near the target.","k":[["Why prefer requests per target over CPU for web services?","It measures load directly, so scaling reacts before CPU saturates and works for IO-bound apps."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-scaling-policies-c2","t":"Step and simple scaling","p":"Step scaling adds or removes a different amount depending on how far a CloudWatch alarm is breached. Simple scaling does one fixed action per alarm with a cooldown.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-scaling-policies-c3","t":"Scaling on queue depth","p":"For workers that read from SQS, the useful signal is backlog per instance, not CPU.","k":[["Which metric would you scale SQS workers on?","Backlog per instance (queue depth divided by instances), not CPU."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-scaling-policies-c4","t":"Scheduled and predictive scaling","p":"Scheduled actions change capacity at set times. Predictive scaling learns daily and weekly patterns and scales ahead of load.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-scaling-policies-c5","t":"Warm-up, cooldown and scale-in","p":"Scaling decisions need time to take effect. Warm-up and cooldown stop the ASG from reacting twice to the same load.","k":[["What happens if warm-up is shorter than boot time?","New instances report low load too early, so the ASG thinks it has surplus capacity and may scale in again."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s22-lifecycle-refresh-warmpools","title":"Lifecycle hooks, instance refresh and warm pools","c":[{"id":"s22-lifecycle-refresh-warmpools-c1","t":"Lifecycle hooks","p":"A lifecycle hook pauses an instance in a wait state when it launches or before it terminates, so your automation can finish its work first.","k":[["What happens when a hook times out?","The ASG applies the DefaultResult (CONTINUE or ABANDON)."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-lifecycle-refresh-warmpools-c2","t":"Instance refresh","p":"Instance refresh replaces instances in an ASG gradually so they pick up a new template version, while keeping a minimum healthy percentage.","k":[["Does updating the launch template version replace running instances?","No. You need an instance refresh (or manual replacement) to roll it out."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-lifecycle-refresh-warmpools-c3","t":"Warm pools","p":"A warm pool keeps pre-initialised instances stopped (or hibernated or running) next to the ASG, so scale-out takes seconds instead of the full boot time.","k":[],"o":[["Smaller, faster image (baked AMI)","any fleet; always first","image is already minimal"],["Warm pool","boot plus init takes minutes and spikes are sharp","scale events are slow and predictable"],["Predictive scaling","repeating daily pattern","traffic is random"],["Over-provision","cannot tolerate any delay","cost is tight"]]},{"id":"s22-lifecycle-refresh-warmpools-c4","t":"Auto Scaling for EKS and Karpenter","p":"EKS managed node groups use ASGs. Karpenter and Cluster Autoscaler decide when nodes are needed from pending pods.","k":[["What triggers node scale-out in Kubernetes?","Pods that cannot be scheduled (Pending), which Cluster Autoscaler or Karpenter react to."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s23-s3-fundamentals-classes","title":"S3 fundamentals and storage classes","c":[{"id":"s23-s3-fundamentals-classes-c1","t":"Buckets, objects and keys","p":"A bucket is a named container in one Region. An object is a blob of data with a key (its full name), metadata and optional tags. There are no real folders; slashes in keys are only a naming convention.","k":[["Does S3 have real folders?","No. Keys are flat strings; the console shows prefixes as folders."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-s3-fundamentals-classes-c2","t":"Secure bucket defaults","p":"New buckets should block public access, enforce ownership by the bucket owner, encrypt data and require TLS.","k":[["What does BucketOwnerEnforced do?","Disables ACLs, so the bucket owner owns every object and policies are the only access control."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-s3-fundamentals-classes-c3","t":"Storage classes","p":"A storage class trades storage price against retrieval cost, retrieval time and minimum duration.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-s3-fundamentals-classes-c4","t":"Restoring archived objects","p":"Objects in Glacier Flexible Retrieval and Deep Archive are not readable until you request a restore, which creates a temporary copy.","k":[["Can you GET a Deep Archive object directly?","No. Restore it first and read the temporary copy."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-s3-fundamentals-classes-c5","t":"Consistency, durability and availability","p":"S3 reads see the latest write immediately. Durability is about not losing data; availability is about reaching it.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s24-s3-lifecycle-versioning","title":"S3 versioning, lifecycle and inventory","c":[{"id":"s24-s3-lifecycle-versioning-c1","t":"Versioning","p":"With versioning on, every write creates a new version and a delete adds a delete marker instead of removing data.","k":[["What does deleting an object in a versioned bucket do?","It adds a delete marker; earlier versions remain and can be restored."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s24-s3-lifecycle-versioning-c2","t":"Lifecycle rules","p":"A lifecycle configuration is a set of rules that move objects to cheaper classes or delete them after a number of days, optionally filtered by prefix, tag or size.","k":[["Why add AbortIncompleteMultipartUpload?","Failed multipart uploads leave hidden parts that you pay for until they are aborted."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s24-s3-lifecycle-versioning-c3","t":"Versioning plus lifecycle in Terraform","p":"Treat bucket configuration as code so that versioning and retention are consistent and reviewed.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s24-s3-lifecycle-versioning-c4","t":"S3 Inventory and Storage Lens","p":"Listing a billion objects through the API is slow and expensive. Inventory delivers a scheduled report file; Storage Lens shows usage and activity trends across accounts.","k":[["Why use Inventory instead of list-objects for huge buckets?","It produces a scheduled bulk report cheaply, avoiding millions of slow, billed LIST calls."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s24-s3-lifecycle-versioning-c5","t":"Batch Operations and cost review","p":"S3 Batch Operations runs one action (copy, tag, restore, invoke a Lambda function, replace ACLs, set Object Lock) across a manifest of objects.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s25-s3-replication-encryption","title":"S3 replication and encryption","c":[{"id":"s25-s3-replication-encryption-c1","t":"Replication","p":"S3 Replication copies new objects asynchronously from a source bucket to one or more destination buckets, in the same Region (SRR) or another (CRR), in the same or another account.","k":[["Are objects that existed before the rule replicated?","No. Use S3 Batch Replication for existing objects."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s25-s3-replication-encryption-c2","t":"Encryption at rest","p":"Every S3 object is encrypted at rest. You choose who manages the key.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s25-s3-replication-encryption-c3","t":"KMS permissions for S3","p":"With SSE-KMS a caller needs permission on the bucket and on the key. A missing key permission looks like an S3 access denied.","k":[["A read fails only for SSE-KMS objects. What do you check first?","The caller's kms:Decrypt permission and the key policy, in addition to the S3 policy."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s25-s3-replication-encryption-c4","t":"Enforcing encryption and transit security","p":"Policies can reject uploads that do not meet your encryption and TLS standards.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s25-s3-replication-encryption-c5","t":"Multi-Region Access Points and replication patterns","p":"A Multi-Region Access Point gives one global endpoint that routes requests to the closest replicated bucket.","k":[["Why must versioning be enabled before replication?","Replication tracks object versions, so both source and destination need versioning."]],"o":[["Cross-Region replication","DR copy, residency","you need one global endpoint for clients"],["Multi-Region Access Point","active-active reads and failover","single Region applications"],["Backups with AWS Backup","scheduled, vault-locked copies","low RPO sync is required"]]}]},{"id":"s26-s3-access-control-features","title":"S3 access control, access points and presigned URLs","c":[{"id":"s26-s3-access-control-features-c1","t":"How S3 authorises requests","p":"S3 combines the caller's IAM policies, the bucket policy, access point policies, Block Public Access, ACLs (if enabled) and any organization SCPs/RCPs. Evaluation follows the IAM logic: explicit deny wins, otherwise some allow is needed.","k":[["Can an object ACL still expose data in a BucketOwnerEnforced bucket?","No. ACLs are disabled, so only policies apply."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-s3-access-control-features-c2","t":"S3 Access Points","p":"An access point is a named endpoint attached to a bucket with its own policy and optional VPC restriction.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-s3-access-control-features-c3","t":"Presigned URLs","p":"A presigned URL carries a signature that lets anyone who holds it perform one specific S3 operation until it expires, without AWS credentials.","k":[["Why use presigned POST instead of PUT for browser uploads?","POST lets you enforce conditions such as maximum size and content type."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-s3-access-control-features-c4","t":"Object Lock and MFA delete","p":"Object Lock makes object versions write-once-read-many (WORM) for a retention period or indefinitely.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-s3-access-control-features-c5","t":"Event notifications and access logging","p":"S3 can announce changes and record access, giving you hooks for automation and audit trails.","k":[["Which logging gives reliable object level auditing?","CloudTrail data events; server access logs are best effort."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s27-s3-performance-kubernetes","title":"S3 performance, transfer and use from Kubernetes","c":[{"id":"s27-s3-performance-kubernetes-c1","t":"Request rates and prefixes","p":"S3 scales automatically per prefix. Each prefix supports thousands of requests per second, and you can use many prefixes in parallel.","k":[["What should clients do on a 503 Slow Down?","Retry with exponential backoff and jitter and spread keys across prefixes."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-s3-performance-kubernetes-c2","t":"Multipart upload and byte-range fetches","p":"Large objects are uploaded in parts in parallel, and downloaded in ranges in parallel.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-s3-performance-kubernetes-c3","t":"Network path and Transfer Acceleration","p":"Where your client sits relative to the bucket decides latency and cost. Transfer Acceleration routes uploads through the nearest CloudFront edge.","k":[["How do you avoid NAT cost for S3 traffic from private subnets?","Add an S3 gateway VPC endpoint and route the subnets to it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-s3-performance-kubernetes-c4","t":"S3 from pods","p":"Pods access S3 using IAM roles through Pod Identity or IRSA. The Mountpoint for Amazon S3 CSI driver can mount a bucket as a file system for read-heavy workloads.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-s3-performance-kubernetes-c5","t":"Choosing an S3 design","p":"Pulling the section together: a short decision flow for a new bucket.","k":[["Which feature protects against an administrator deleting audit logs?","Object Lock in compliance mode (with retention)."]],"o":[["Versioning plus lifecycle","any important data","you need immutability against admins"],["Add Object Lock","compliance or ransomware protection","data must be deletable at will"],["Add cross-Region replication","regional disaster recovery","data residency forbids copies"]]}]},{"id":"s28-lambda-execution-scaling","title":"Lambda execution model and scaling","c":[{"id":"s28-lambda-execution-scaling-c1","t":"The execution environment lifecycle","p":"Lambda creates an isolated execution environment (a Firecracker micro VM) for your function, runs your code in it, and reuses it for later requests until it is retired.","k":[["Where should you create SDK clients?","Outside the handler so warm invocations reuse them."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-lambda-execution-scaling-c2","t":"Cold starts","p":"A cold start is the extra latency of the init phase when no warm environment is available.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-lambda-execution-scaling-c3","t":"Memory, CPU and timeout","p":"You pick memory from 128 MB up to 10,240 MB. CPU and network scale in proportion to memory. The timeout can be up to 15 minutes.","k":[["What two numbers multiply to give GB-seconds?","Memory in GB and billed duration in seconds, per invocation, summed over all invocations."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-lambda-execution-scaling-c4","t":"Concurrency and throttling","p":"Concurrency is the number of environments serving requests at the same moment. It is roughly requests per second multiplied by average duration in seconds.","k":[["How do you estimate required concurrency?","Requests per second times average duration in seconds."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-lambda-execution-scaling-c5","t":"Provisioned concurrency","p":"Provisioned concurrency keeps a chosen number of environments initialised and ready, so those requests avoid cold starts.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-lambda-execution-scaling-c6","t":"Versions and aliases","p":"Publishing creates an immutable version of code and configuration. An alias is a named pointer to a version, and can split traffic between two versions.","k":[["What does a function URL or API call normally target, `$LATEST` or an alias?","An alias, so deployments are controlled and reversible."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-lambda-execution-scaling-c7","t":"Execution role and resource policy","p":"Two different policies apply. The execution role says what the function may call. The resource-based policy says who may invoke the function.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s29-lambda-packaging-events-ops","title":"Lambda packaging, event sources and operations","c":[{"id":"s29-lambda-packaging-events-ops-c1","t":"Zip packages and layers","p":"A zip package holds your code and dependencies. A layer is a separate zip that is mounted under /opt and shared between functions.","k":[["Does publishing a new layer version update the functions that use it?","No. Each function must be updated to reference the new version."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s29-lambda-packaging-events-ops-c2","t":"Container image functions","p":"Lambda can run a container image up to 10 GB stored in Amazon ECR instead of a zip.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s29-lambda-packaging-events-ops-c3","t":"Synchronous, asynchronous and polling invocation","p":"There are three ways Lambda is invoked, and each has different retry behaviour.","k":[["Which invocation types retry automatically inside the Lambda service?","Asynchronous and polling. Synchronous callers must retry themselves."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s29-lambda-packaging-events-ops-c4","t":"SQS and stream event source mappings","p":"An event source mapping is a Lambda-managed poller that reads a queue or stream and invokes your function with batches.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s29-lambda-packaging-events-ops-c5","t":"Function URLs and Lambda in a VPC","p":"A function URL is a built-in HTTPS endpoint. A function can also attach to your VPC to reach private resources.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s29-lambda-packaging-events-ops-c6","t":"SnapStart and runtime choices","p":"SnapStart takes a snapshot of an initialised environment after the init phase and restores it for new environments, cutting cold start time.","k":[["Why must uniqueness-sensitive values be created after restore?","The snapshot is copied to many environments, so values made during init would be shared."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s29-lambda-packaging-events-ops-c7","t":"Observability and cost control","p":"Lambda writes logs to CloudWatch Logs automatically, emits metrics, and supports X-Ray and OpenTelemetry tracing.","k":[["What two default behaviours of Lambda logs can quietly raise cost?","Log groups keep data forever and verbose logging per invocation adds ingestion charges."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s30-api-gateway-appsync","title":"API Gateway and AppSync","c":[{"id":"s30-api-gateway-appsync-c1","t":"The API products compared","p":"API Gateway offers REST APIs, HTTP APIs and WebSocket APIs. AppSync offers managed GraphQL.","k":[["Which product gives usage plans with API keys?","The REST API type."]],"o":[["HTTP API","simple proxy to Lambda or a service and JWT auth","you need API keys, usage plans or WAF directly on the API"],["REST API","you need usage plans, WAF, caching or private APIs","you only need a cheap proxy"],["AppSync","clients need flexible queries and live subscriptions","a simple fixed REST contract is enough"]]},{"id":"s30-api-gateway-appsync-c2","t":"Integrations and request flow","p":"An integration connects a route to a backend. The gateway receives the request, authorises it, applies throttling, calls the backend and returns the answer.","k":[["Why do long jobs not belong behind a synchronous API integration?","The integration timeout is short, so return 202 and process asynchronously."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s30-api-gateway-appsync-c3","t":"Authentication and authorisation","p":"API Gateway can verify callers with IAM, Cognito or JWT tokens, or call your own Lambda authorizer.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s30-api-gateway-appsync-c4","t":"Throttling, usage plans and WAF","p":"Throttling limits request rates. On REST APIs, usage plans attach rate and quota limits to API keys. WAF blocks malicious traffic.","k":[["Is an API key a good way to authenticate users?","No. It identifies a client for metering. Use IAM or JWT for authentication."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s30-api-gateway-appsync-c5","t":"Stages, deployments and custom domains","p":"A stage is a named, deployed version of an API such as prod. A custom domain with an ACM certificate maps your own hostname to stages.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s30-api-gateway-appsync-c6","t":"Private APIs and VPC links","p":"A private REST API is reachable only through an interface VPC endpoint. A VPC link lets any API call private backends.","k":[["What does a VPC link need to reach a private ALB?","ENIs in your subnets and a security group allowed by the ALB or targets."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s30-api-gateway-appsync-c7","t":"AppSync and GraphQL","p":"AppSync is managed GraphQL. Clients ask for exactly the fields they need in one call, and can subscribe to changes.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s30-api-gateway-appsync-c8","t":"Troubleshooting API errors","p":"Most API Gateway failures come from a short list: permissions, timeouts, malformed responses and throttling.","k":[["A Lambda proxy returns a plain string and API Gateway gives 500. Why?","The proxy integration expects a structured response with statusCode and body."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s31-step-functions","title":"AWS Step Functions","c":[{"id":"s31-step-functions-c1","t":"What a state machine is","p":"A state machine is a JSON definition of steps (states), the order between them and the data passed along. Each run is an execution.","k":[["What language defines a Step Functions workflow?","Amazon States Language, a JSON format."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s31-step-functions-c2","t":"Standard versus Express workflows","p":"Standard workflows run up to a year with exactly-once step semantics. Express workflows run up to five minutes at very high rates with at-least-once semantics.","k":[["Which type needs idempotent steps because they may repeat?","Express."]],"o":[["Standard","long running, human approval, audit needs","millions of short events per hour with tight cost"],["Express","high volume short processing, API backends","you need to wait days or need exactly-once"]]},{"id":"s31-step-functions-c3","t":"Service integrations and patterns","p":"Step Functions can call over 200 AWS services directly (SDK integrations) without Lambda glue code.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s31-step-functions-c4","t":"Errors, retries and catch","p":"Each Task can declare how to Retry on named errors and where to go on failure with Catch.","k":[["Sum the waits for interval 2 s, rate 2.0, 4 retries.","2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 30 seconds if all four retries occur."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s31-step-functions-c5","t":"Parallel, Map and Distributed Map","p":"Parallel runs different branches at once. Map runs the same steps for each item of a list. Distributed Map processes huge datasets such as S3 objects in parallel child executions.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s31-step-functions-c6","t":"Saga pattern and human approval","p":"A saga undoes completed steps when a later step fails. Step Functions expresses this with Catch states that run compensation tasks.","k":[["How does a human approval step pause a workflow?","A waitForTaskToken task hands out a token and resumes when SendTaskSuccess or SendTaskFailure is called with it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s31-step-functions-c7","t":"Operating Step Functions","p":"Operate workflows with logging, alarms, tracing and infrastructure as code.","k":[["Which action restarts a failed Standard execution from its failed step?","Redrive."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s32-eventbridge","title":"Amazon EventBridge","c":[{"id":"s32-eventbridge-c1","t":"Events, buses and rules","p":"An event is a JSON record of something that happened. An event bus receives events. A rule matches events by pattern and sends them to targets.","k":[["Does the producer need to know the consumers?","No. It only publishes to the bus."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s32-eventbridge-c2","t":"Event patterns","p":"An event pattern is a JSON document that lists the fields and values an event must have to match.","k":[["Within one field, are listed values AND or OR?","OR. Different fields are combined with AND."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s32-eventbridge-c3","t":"Targets, input transformation and retries","p":"Each target can reshape the event with an input transformer, and EventBridge retries failed deliveries before giving up.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s32-eventbridge-c4","t":"Cross-account and cross-Region buses","p":"A bus can receive events from other accounts and Regions and forward events to another bus.","k":[["How do you let every account in your organization send to a bus?","A bus resource policy with an aws:PrincipalOrgID condition."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s32-eventbridge-c5","t":"Archive and replay, schema registry","p":"An archive stores events from a bus so you can replay them later. The schema registry discovers and stores event shapes.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s32-eventbridge-c6","t":"EventBridge Pipes","p":"A pipe connects one source to one target, with optional filtering, enrichment and transformation in between.","k":[["Bus or pipe for one queue feeding one workflow?","A pipe."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s32-eventbridge-c7","t":"EventBridge Scheduler","p":"Scheduler runs one-time or recurring schedules using cron, rate or at() expressions and calls over 270 services directly.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s32-eventbridge-c8","t":"API destinations and operations","p":"API destinations let a rule call any HTTPS endpoint with managed authentication and rate limiting.","k":[["Where are OAuth secrets for an API destination kept?","In a connection, stored by EventBridge in Secrets Manager."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s33-sqs-sns","title":"SQS and SNS messaging","c":[{"id":"s33-sqs-sns-c1","t":"SQS basics","p":"SQS is a managed queue. Producers send messages, consumers poll and delete them after processing.","k":[["Why use long polling?","It reduces empty receives, lowering cost and latency."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s33-sqs-sns-c2","t":"Visibility timeout","p":"When a consumer receives a message it becomes invisible for the visibility timeout. If it is not deleted in time, it becomes visible again and another consumer may get it.","k":[["Lambda timeout is 30 s. What visibility timeout does AWS recommend at least?","180 seconds, six times 30."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s33-sqs-sns-c3","t":"Standard versus FIFO queues","p":"Standard queues give very high throughput with at-least-once delivery and best-effort ordering. FIFO queues keep order per message group and support deduplication.","k":[],"o":[["Standard","throughput matters and handlers are idempotent","order is a hard requirement"],["FIFO","order per entity such as an account","you need huge parallelism in one group"]]},{"id":"s33-sqs-sns-c4","t":"Dead letter queues and redrive","p":"A dead letter queue (DLQ) is another queue that receives messages that failed too many times.","k":[["Why give the DLQ longer retention?","The message age counts from first enqueue, so a short DLQ retention could expire messages immediately."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s33-sqs-sns-c5","t":"SNS topics and subscriptions","p":"SNS publishes a message to a topic, and every subscriber receives a copy.","k":[["Which feature stops a subscriber receiving irrelevant messages?","A subscription filter policy."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s33-sqs-sns-c6","t":"Fan-out policy and permissions","p":"SNS needs permission to send to a queue, and the queue policy is where most fan-out setups fail.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s33-sqs-sns-c7","t":"Choosing SQS, SNS, EventBridge and Kinesis","p":"All four move data between components, but with different delivery models.","k":[["Which service retains a stream so a new consumer can read from the past?","Kinesis (or MSK). EventBridge offers archive and replay of events."]],"o":[["SQS","you need a buffer and retries for one worker pool","many independent subscribers need copies"],["SNS","simple push to several endpoints","you need replay or complex routing"],["EventBridge","routing across services and accounts","you need very high throughput of ordered records"],["Kinesis","ordered high volume records and several readers","a simple task queue is enough"]]}]},{"id":"s34-ecs-fargate-tasks","title":"ECS and Fargate tasks","c":[{"id":"s34-ecs-fargate-tasks-c1","t":"ECS building blocks","p":"A cluster groups capacity. A task definition describes containers. A task is a running instance of it. A service keeps a desired number of tasks running.","k":[["What keeps a number of tasks running and replaces failed ones?","An ECS service."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s34-ecs-fargate-tasks-c2","t":"Task definition anatomy","p":"The task definition lists containers, images, CPU and memory, ports, environment, secrets, logging and roles.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s34-ecs-fargate-tasks-c3","t":"Task role versus execution role","p":"Two roles apply. The execution role lets ECS pull the image, write logs and fetch secrets. The task role is what your application code uses to call AWS.","k":[["The task cannot pull the image from ECR. Which role is missing a permission?","The execution role."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s34-ecs-fargate-tasks-c4","t":"Fargate networking and sizing","p":"Each Fargate task gets its own ENI and private IP in your subnet (awsvpc), with its own security group.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s34-ecs-fargate-tasks-c5","t":"Fargate versus EC2 launch type","p":"Fargate runs tasks without you managing servers. The EC2 launch type runs tasks on your own instances.","k":[["Which launch type supports privileged containers?","EC2, not Fargate."]],"o":[["Fargate","small team and variable load","you need GPUs or privileged mode"],["EC2","steady high utilisation or special hardware","nobody can patch and scale instances"]]},{"id":"s34-ecs-fargate-tasks-c6","t":"Logging, metrics and ECS Exec","p":"ECS sends container output to CloudWatch Logs and metrics to CloudWatch; ECS Exec opens a shell in a running container using SSM.","k":[["What does a CannotPullContainerError usually point to?","Missing network path to the registry or missing execution role permission."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s35-ecs-services-apprunner","title":"ECS services, discovery and App Runner","c":[{"id":"s35-ecs-services-apprunner-c1","t":"ECS services and deployments","p":"A service keeps the desired number of tasks running and replaces failed ones. It also performs rolling or blue/green deployments of new task definition revisions.","k":[["Desired 4, min healthy 100, max 200. How many tasks can run at once during deploy?","Up to 8."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s35-ecs-services-apprunner-c2","t":"Service auto scaling","p":"Application Auto Scaling changes a service's desired count based on metrics.","k":[["Which metric scales on traffic share per task behind an ALB?","ALBRequestCountPerTarget."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s35-ecs-services-apprunner-c3","t":"Capacity providers","p":"A capacity provider tells a cluster where tasks may run: Fargate, Fargate Spot or an Auto Scaling group of EC2 instances.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s35-ecs-services-apprunner-c4","t":"Service discovery and Service Connect","p":"Services find each other by name. Cloud Map gives DNS names; ECS Service Connect adds a managed proxy with retries, metrics and short names.","k":[["Why not use task IPs directly?","They change on every task replacement."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s35-ecs-services-apprunner-c5","t":"ECS versus EKS","p":"ECS is simple and AWS specific. EKS is Kubernetes, portable, with a large ecosystem and more to operate.","k":[["Name two reasons to choose EKS over ECS.","Kubernetes ecosystem and portability (Helm, operators, CRDs); existing Kubernetes skills."]],"o":[["ECS on Fargate","small team and plain web services","you need CRDs, operators or Helm charts"],["EKS","you already use Kubernetes tooling and need portability","nobody owns cluster upgrades"],["App Runner","a single web service from image or source","you need sidecars or private non-HTTP services"]]},{"id":"s35-ecs-services-apprunner-c6","t":"AWS App Runner","p":"App Runner builds or pulls your web app and runs it with HTTPS, scaling and a load balancer, with almost no configuration.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s35-ecs-services-apprunner-c7","t":"ECS troubleshooting","p":"Most ECS issues sit in a few places: image pull, health checks, memory limits and permissions.","k":[["A task exits with code 137. What is the first suspect?","Out of memory kill; check memory limits and usage."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s36-efs-fsx","title":"EFS and FSx file storage","c":[{"id":"s36-efs-fsx-c1","t":"Storage types compared","p":"Block (EBS) attaches to one instance. File (EFS, FSx) is shared over NFS or SMB. Object (S3) is accessed over HTTP API.","k":[["Which service gives shared NFS across AZs with elastic size?","EFS."]],"o":[["EFS","many Linux clients need a growing shared POSIX filesystem","you need SMB or extreme parallel throughput"],["FSx for Windows","Windows apps need SMB and Active Directory","workloads are Linux only"],["FSx for Lustre","HPC or ML needs very high throughput with S3 linkage","you need long term durable general storage"],["S3","files are written once and read as objects","applications require file locking and POSIX"]]},{"id":"s36-efs-fsx-c2","t":"EFS architecture and storage classes","p":"EFS is a regional NFS 4.x file system with mount targets in each AZ subnet. Capacity grows and shrinks automatically.","k":[["Which port must the mount target security group allow?","TCP 2049 (NFS)."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s36-efs-fsx-c3","t":"EFS performance and throughput modes","p":"EFS has performance behaviour set by its throughput mode: Elastic, Provisioned or Bursting.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s36-efs-fsx-c4","t":"Access points and permissions","p":"An access point is an application entry into an EFS file system that enforces a root directory and POSIX identity.","k":[["How can two apps share one EFS yet stay isolated?","Give each its own access point with a distinct path and POSIX identity."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s36-efs-fsx-c5","t":"FSx for Windows and NetApp ONTAP","p":"FSx for Windows File Server offers SMB shares with Active Directory. FSx for NetApp ONTAP offers NFS, SMB and iSCSI with NetApp features.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s36-efs-fsx-c6","t":"FSx for Lustre and OpenZFS","p":"FSx for Lustre is a high performance parallel file system for HPC and machine learning. FSx for OpenZFS gives NFS with ZFS snapshots and clones.","k":[["Which FSx variant is meant for HPC and ML with S3 integration?","FSx for Lustre."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s36-efs-fsx-c7","t":"Backups, cost and operating file systems","p":"Protect shared files with backups, watch growth and pick storage tiers to control cost.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s37-kinesis-msk","title":"Streaming with Kinesis and MSK","c":[{"id":"s37-kinesis-msk-c1","t":"Streaming fundamentals","p":"A stream is an append-only log of records split into partitions. Producers write, consumers read from their own position.","k":[["What guarantees order for related records?","Writing them with the same partition key so they land in one shard or partition."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s37-kinesis-msk-c2","t":"Kinesis Data Streams shards and capacity","p":"A shard accepts up to 1 MB per second or 1,000 records per second of writes and delivers up to 2 MB per second of reads (check the current quotas).","k":[["Peak is 6 MB per second at 1 MB per shard. How many shards at minimum?","6."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s37-kinesis-msk-c3","t":"Consumers and enhanced fan-out","p":"Consumers read with the shared 2 MB per second per shard limit, or get a dedicated 2 MB per second each with enhanced fan-out.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s37-kinesis-msk-c4","t":"Firehose delivery","p":"Amazon Data Firehose loads streaming data into S3, Redshift, OpenSearch, Splunk and HTTP endpoints, with buffering and optional transformation.","k":[["What two triggers flush a Firehose buffer?","Buffer size and buffer interval, whichever comes first."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s37-kinesis-msk-c5","t":"Amazon MSK","p":"Amazon MSK runs Apache Kafka brokers for you, in your VPC, with Kafka protocol compatibility.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s37-kinesis-msk-c6","t":"Choosing Kinesis, MSK, SQS and EventBridge","p":"Pick by the access pattern, the ecosystem you already use and the operations you accept.","k":[["Which option is best to just land logs in S3 as Parquet?","Firehose."]],"o":[["Kinesis Data Streams","AWS native producers and Lambda consumers","you must reuse Kafka clients and connectors"],["MSK","you run Kafka today or need its ecosystem","the team cannot operate Kafka concepts"],["Firehose","the goal is only to land data in S3 or OpenSearch","you need custom consumers and replay"]]}]},{"id":"s38-athena-glue-lakeformation","title":"Athena, Glue and Lake Formation","c":[{"id":"s38-athena-glue-lakeformation-c1","t":"Athena serverless SQL","p":"Athena runs SQL directly on files in S3 with no servers, and bills by data scanned.","k":[["What does Athena bill on?","The amount of data scanned by each query."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s38-athena-glue-lakeformation-c2","t":"Cutting scan cost with partitions and Parquet","p":"Store data in a columnar format (Parquet) and in partitioned folders so a query reads only the columns and days it needs.","k":[["Daily partitions of 10 GB for a year: scan for one day?","10 GB, versus 3,650 GB without pruning."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s38-athena-glue-lakeformation-c3","t":"Glue Data Catalog and crawlers","p":"The Glue Data Catalog is a central metadata store of databases and tables. Crawlers scan data and create or update table definitions.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s38-athena-glue-lakeformation-c4","t":"Glue ETL jobs","p":"Glue jobs run Apache Spark or Python scripts on managed workers to clean and convert data.","k":[["What does a job bookmark do?","Remembers what was processed so reruns handle only new data."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s38-athena-glue-lakeformation-c5","t":"Lake Formation permissions","p":"Lake Formation adds database-style grants (table, column, row) on top of the Glue catalog, instead of writing S3 bucket policies per user.","k":[["How would you hide a PII column from analysts?","Grant SELECT on only the allowed columns, or use LF-tags with a sensitivity classification."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s38-athena-glue-lakeformation-c6","t":"Iceberg tables and the lakehouse","p":"Apache Iceberg is an open table format giving a data lake ACID updates, schema evolution and time travel.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s38-athena-glue-lakeformation-c7","t":"Data lake zones and cost governance","p":"Organise the lake into raw, cleaned and curated zones with clear ownership, lifecycle and access rules.","k":[["What stops a single query scanning terabytes by mistake?","A workgroup scan cutoff per query."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s39-opensearch-service","title":"Amazon OpenSearch Service","c":[{"id":"s39-opensearch-service-c1","t":"When to use OpenSearch","p":"OpenSearch indexes JSON documents so you can run full-text search, filters, aggregations and vector search quickly.","k":[["Is OpenSearch a good system of record?","No. Keep the source of truth in a database and treat the index as a derived copy."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s39-opensearch-service-c2","t":"Domains, nodes, shards and replicas","p":"A domain is a cluster. Indexes are split into primary shards, each with replica copies on other nodes.","k":[["Can you change primary shard count of an existing index freely?","No. It is fixed at creation; reindex, split or shrink to change it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s39-opensearch-service-c3","t":"Sizing storage and instances","p":"Size by raw data per day, retention, replicas and overhead, then choose instance types for memory and IOPS.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s39-opensearch-service-c4","t":"Network and access security","p":"Put the domain in a VPC, use fine-grained access control and a resource policy, and encrypt everything.","k":[["Why prefer a VPC domain?","It has no public endpoint, reducing exposure."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s39-opensearch-service-c5","t":"Index lifecycle and snapshots","p":"Use Index State Management policies to roll over, move, and delete indexes on a schedule. Snapshots protect against loss.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s39-opensearch-service-c6","t":"OpenSearch Serverless and operations","p":"OpenSearch Serverless removes node and shard management: you create a collection and pay for compute (OCUs) and storage.","k":[["Which status means some primary shard is unassigned?","Red."]],"o":[["Managed domain","steady load and you need tuning control","nobody can size and patch clusters"],["Serverless","variable load and a small team","you need features not yet supported there"]]}]},{"id":"s40-rds-ha-replicas","title":"RDS high availability and replicas","c":[{"id":"s40-rds-ha-replicas-c1","t":"What RDS manages and what you still own","p":"RDS gives you a database endpoint without giving you the operating system. You choose the engine, version, instance class and storage; AWS runs the host, the install, backups and failover automation.","k":[["Which part of the database can you not reach on RDS?","The operating system of the host; you use parameter groups and the service APIs instead."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s40-rds-ha-replicas-c2","t":"Multi-AZ instance deployment","p":"RDS keeps a second, hidden copy of your database in another Availability Zone. Every write is copied to it synchronously. If the primary fails, RDS points the same DNS name at the copy.","k":[["Can you send read queries to the standby of a Multi-AZ instance?","No. Use a read replica or a Multi-AZ DB cluster for readable standbys."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s40-rds-ha-replicas-c3","t":"Multi-AZ DB cluster deployment","p":"A newer RDS layout for MySQL and PostgreSQL with one writer and two readable standbys in three AZs. Commits are acknowledged when at least one standby has the data.","k":[],"o":[["Multi-AZ instance","any engine, simple HA, cost matters","you need to read from the standby"],["Multi-AZ DB cluster","MySQL or PostgreSQL with read scaling and quick failover","you need Oracle or SQL Server"],["Aurora","storage-level replication, up to 15 readers, fastest failover","you need an engine Aurora does not offer"]]},{"id":"s40-rds-ha-replicas-c4","t":"Read replicas","p":"A read replica is another database instance that continuously receives changes from a source instance asynchronously. You send reporting and read-heavy queries to it.","k":[["Why is a read replica not a substitute for backups?","Replication is continuous, so a bad write or drop is copied to the replica within seconds."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s40-rds-ha-replicas-c5","t":"Parameter groups and option groups","p":"A parameter group is a named set of engine settings such as maxconnections or innodbbufferpoolsize. You attach it to an instance because you cannot edit config files on the host.","k":[["What does a status of pending-reboot mean?","A static parameter was changed and takes effect only after the instance reboots."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s40-rds-ha-replicas-c6","t":"Subnet groups, security groups and encryption","p":"A DB subnet group tells RDS which private subnets (at least two AZs) it may place instances in. Security groups decide who can connect. Encryption at rest uses KMS and is chosen when you create the database.","k":[["How do you encrypt an existing unencrypted RDS database?","Copy a snapshot with a KMS key, then restore a new instance from the encrypted copy and cut over."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s41-rds-backup-proxy","title":"RDS backups, PITR, upgrades and RDS Proxy","c":[{"id":"s41-rds-backup-proxy-c1","t":"Automated backups and retention","p":"RDS takes a daily storage snapshot during your backup window and continuously uploads transaction logs to S3. Together they allow recovery to any second in the retention period.","k":[["What happens to automated backups when you delete the instance?","They are removed after deletion unless you retain them; take a final or manual snapshot first."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s41-rds-backup-proxy-c2","t":"Point-in-time recovery","p":"PITR rebuilds your database as it was at a chosen second. It always creates a new instance; it never rewinds the existing one.","k":[["Does PITR overwrite the original instance?","No, it creates a new instance with a new endpoint."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s41-rds-backup-proxy-c3","t":"Manual snapshots, copies and sharing","p":"A manual snapshot is a backup you create and keep until you delete it. You can copy it to another Region or share it with another account.","k":[["Why can a snapshot encrypted with the default aws/rds key not be shared?","The AWS-managed key policy cannot be edited to grant another account; use a customer managed key."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s41-rds-backup-proxy-c4","t":"Maintenance, minor upgrades and blue/green deployments","p":"RDS patches in a weekly maintenance window. For major upgrades or risky changes, a blue/green deployment builds a synchronized copy (green), lets you change and test it, and then switches over.","k":[["How does blue/green avoid application changes?","Switchover swaps the endpoint names so the same hostnames point at the new environment."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s41-rds-backup-proxy-c5","t":"RDS Proxy","p":"RDS Proxy is a managed connection pool that sits between your application and the database. Applications connect to the proxy; the proxy keeps a small set of warm connections to the database.","k":[["What is connection pinning?","A client is locked to one database connection because of session state, so the proxy cannot share that connection with others."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s41-rds-backup-proxy-c6","t":"Monitoring RDS","p":"Three layers show database health: CloudWatch metrics from the hypervisor, Enhanced Monitoring from an OS agent, and Performance Insights which shows which SQL consumes time.","k":[["What does DB Load in Performance Insights measure?","The average number of active sessions, broken down by wait event, SQL statement, user or host."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s42-aurora-storage-failover","title":"Aurora architecture, storage and failover","c":[{"id":"s42-aurora-storage-failover-c1","t":"Aurora compared with RDS","p":"In RDS each instance owns its own EBS volume. In Aurora, all instances in a cluster share one distributed storage volume that spans three AZs. Instances only do compute.","k":[["Why is Aurora replica lag small?","Readers read the same shared storage volume instead of applying a replicated log to their own disks."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s42-aurora-storage-failover-c2","t":"Quorum storage and self-healing","p":"Every 10 GiB chunk of data is stored six times, two in each of three AZs. A write succeeds when four of six copies confirm; a read needs three.","k":[["How many copies of data does Aurora keep and in how many AZs?","Six copies across three AZs."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s42-aurora-storage-failover-c3","t":"Endpoints","p":"An Aurora cluster publishes several DNS names. Use the right one so writes reach the writer and reads spread over readers.","k":[["Should an application connect to an instance endpoint for writes?","No; the writer role moves on failover, so use the cluster endpoint."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s42-aurora-storage-failover-c4","t":"Failover mechanics","p":"If the writer fails, Aurora promotes a reader to be the new writer. No data copy is needed because storage is shared, so it is much faster than RDS.","k":[["Which reader is promoted first?","The one with the lowest promotion tier; ties go to the largest instance."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s42-aurora-storage-failover-c5","t":"Aurora features worth knowing","p":"Aurora adds operations features that RDS lacks: fast cloning, backtrack (MySQL), parallel query, and the I/O-Optimized storage configuration.","k":[["What does a clone cost initially?","Almost nothing for storage; you pay for changed pages and the clone's instances."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s42-aurora-storage-failover-c6","t":"Aurora with RDS Proxy and connection strategy","p":"Aurora failover is fast, but clients are not. Pools must detect the change, reconnect and retry.","k":[["Why set a max connection lifetime?","So pooled connections are recycled and eventually follow DNS to the new writer after failover."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s43-aurora-global-serverless","title":"Aurora Global Database and Serverless v2","c":[{"id":"s43-aurora-global-serverless-c1","t":"Global Database","p":"A Global Database is one primary Aurora cluster in one Region and up to several read-only secondary clusters in other Regions. Storage-level replication copies changes between them.","k":[["How does Global Database replicate?","At the storage layer, not through the database engine, so the writer is not slowed."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s43-aurora-global-serverless-c2","t":"Switchover versus failover","p":"Planned switchover moves the primary role without data loss. Unplanned failover promotes a secondary after an outage and may lose a few seconds of writes.","k":[["What distinguishes switchover from failover?","Switchover is planned with no data loss; failover is for outages and may lose the unreplicated tail."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s43-aurora-global-serverless-c3","t":"Serverless v2 capacity model","p":"Serverless v2 instances scale their CPU and memory continuously between a minimum and maximum number of Aurora Capacity Units (ACUs). One ACU is about 2 GiB of memory with matching CPU and networking.","k":[["What does the minimum ACU influence besides cost?","The buffer cache size and how quickly the database can absorb a sudden burst."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s43-aurora-global-serverless-c4","t":"Choosing capacity and avoiding surprises","p":"Serverless v2 is flexible, but a badly chosen range gives either high bills or throttled performance.","k":[["Why can Serverless v2 produce \"too many connections\" at a low maximum?","The default connection limit scales with the maximum ACU setting."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s43-aurora-global-serverless-c5","t":"Choosing between RDS, Aurora and the rest","p":"Pick the database by access pattern, scale and cost, not by habit.","k":[],"o":[["RDS Multi-AZ","a standard relational app of moderate size","you need more than five readers or multi-Region writes"],["Aurora provisioned","steady heavy OLTP with many readers","the workload is tiny and cost-sensitive"],["Aurora Serverless v2","spiky, unpredictable, or many dev databases","constant 24/7 high load where reservations win"],["DynamoDB","key-based access, huge scale, no joins","you need ad hoc SQL analytics"]]}]},{"id":"s44-dynamodb-elasticache","title":"DynamoDB and ElastiCache","c":[{"id":"s44-dynamodb-elasticache-c1","t":"DynamoDB tables and keys","p":"DynamoDB is a fully managed key-value and document database. Each item is found by its partition key, and optionally a sort key that orders items sharing the same partition key.","k":[["Why avoid Scan?","It reads every item, consuming capacity proportional to the table size."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s44-dynamodb-elasticache-c2","t":"Capacity modes and throttling","p":"You pay for reads and writes in one of two modes: on-demand (per request) or provisioned (capacity units per second, optionally with auto scaling).","k":[["How much does an eventually consistent 4 KB read cost compared with strongly consistent?","Half of one read capacity unit."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s44-dynamodb-elasticache-c3","t":"Indexes, TTL and streams","p":"Secondary indexes give alternative query keys. TTL deletes expired items for free. Streams emit a change record for each write.","k":[["Can you add an LSI to an existing table?","No; LSIs must be defined at table creation."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s44-dynamodb-elasticache-c4","t":"Global tables and access from EKS","p":"A global table replicates a DynamoDB table across Regions with multi-writer semantics. Pods access DynamoDB through IAM roles.","k":[["How are write conflicts resolved in global tables?","Last writer wins, based on timestamps."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s44-dynamodb-elasticache-c5","t":"ElastiCache for Redis and Valkey","p":"ElastiCache runs in-memory stores (Valkey, Redis OSS, Memcached) as a managed service. Applications use them to hold hot data and sessions with sub-millisecond reads.","k":[["What happens to the data if the cache cluster is lost?","Nothing authoritative is lost, since the database is the source of truth; the cache is rebuilt on demand."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s45-eks-control-plane","title":"EKS control plane and cluster creation","c":[{"id":"s45-eks-control-plane-c1","t":"What EKS manages","p":"EKS runs the Kubernetes control plane (API servers, etcd, controller manager, scheduler) in an AWS-managed account, spread over multiple AZs. You pay a fixed hourly fee per cluster and manage worker capacity separately.","k":[["Where do you find etcd on an EKS cluster?","You do not; it runs in the AWS managed account and is not accessible to you."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s45-eks-control-plane-c2","t":"Creating a cluster with eksctl","p":"eksctl reads a YAML file and creates the cluster, its IAM roles, node groups and optional VPC through CloudFormation.","k":[["Why does the config enable OIDC?","IRSA needs an IAM OIDC identity provider for the cluster so pods can assume roles."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s45-eks-control-plane-c3","t":"Creating a cluster with Terraform","p":"The community module terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws creates the cluster, IAM, security groups and node groups from variables.","k":[["Which subnets should nodes use?","Private subnets, with egress through NAT gateways or VPC endpoints."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s45-eks-control-plane-c4","t":"API endpoint access","p":"The Kubernetes API endpoint can be public, private or both. Private means it resolves to the ENIs in your VPC and is reachable only from the VPC or connected networks.","k":[["Do nodes need the public endpoint to join?","No; with private access enabled they reach the API through the VPC."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s45-eks-control-plane-c5","t":"Control plane logging and audit","p":"EKS can send five log types to CloudWatch Logs: api, audit, authenticator, controllerManager and scheduler.","k":[["Which log type helps debug IAM identity to Kubernetes user mapping?","The authenticator log."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s45-eks-control-plane-c6","t":"Cluster tags, networking requirements and sizing","p":"EKS and its controllers find subnets through tags, and the cluster needs enough free IP addresses.","k":[["What happens if subnets lack the elb tags?","The Load Balancer Controller cannot auto-discover subnets and provisioning fails unless you list subnets explicitly."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s46-eks-compute","title":"EKS compute with node groups and Fargate","c":[{"id":"s46-eks-compute-c1","t":"Managed node groups","p":"A managed node group is an Auto Scaling group of EC2 instances that EKS creates, registers and upgrades for you. Nodes are normal EC2 instances in your account.","k":[["Who creates the EC2 instances of a managed group?","EKS, through an Auto Scaling group in your account."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s46-eks-compute-c2","t":"Node AMIs and operating systems","p":"EKS provides optimized AMIs: Amazon Linux 2023, Bottlerocket (a minimal container OS), Windows, and GPU/accelerated variants.","k":[["Why use SSM Session Manager for node access?","It needs no inbound ports or SSH keys and logs sessions through IAM and CloudTrail."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s46-eks-compute-c3","t":"Updating node groups","p":"Updating a group replaces its nodes with new ones using a newer AMI or Kubernetes version, one batch at a time.","k":[["What stops a drain from completing?","A PodDisruptionBudget that cannot be satisfied, or pods without a controller to recreate them."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s46-eks-compute-c4","t":"Fargate","p":"Fargate runs each pod in its own small, AWS-managed micro VM. There are no nodes for you to patch or scale.","k":[["Can a DaemonSet run on Fargate?","No; each pod has its own micro VM and DaemonSets are not supported."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s46-eks-compute-c5","t":"Choosing compute","p":"Match compute to the workload, and keep a small stable system node group even if you use Karpenter.","k":[],"o":[["Managed node group","stable baseline, system add-ons, predictable pools","you want per-pod right sizing and fast scale"],["Karpenter","variable workloads and cost optimisation","you need only a handful of fixed nodes"],["Fargate","isolated jobs, no node ops","you need DaemonSets, GPUs or EBS"],["Spot","stateless, interruption tolerant","databases and single replicas"]]}]},{"id":"s47-eks-vpc-cni","title":"EKS networking with the VPC CNI","c":[{"id":"s47-eks-vpc-cni-c1","t":"How the VPC CNI assigns IPs","p":"Each node has one or more ENIs. Each ENI has secondary private IPs. The CNI daemon (aws-node) hands one of those IPs to every pod on the node.","k":[["Why can a pod fail with \"failed to assign an IP address\"?","The subnet or the node's ENI limits have no free IPs left."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s47-eks-vpc-cni-c2","t":"Prefix delegation","p":"Instead of assigning single IPs to an ENI, EKS assigns /28 prefixes (16 addresses each), multiplying how many pods a node can run.","k":[["What does a /28 prefix provide?","16 IPv4 addresses for pods on one ENI slot."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s47-eks-vpc-cni-c3","t":"Custom networking and IPv6","p":"When the main subnets are exhausted, custom networking places pod ENIs in a secondary CIDR (like 100.64.0.0/16). IPv6 clusters avoid the problem entirely.","k":[["Can an existing IPv4 cluster be converted to IPv6?","No; the IP family is fixed at creation."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s47-eks-vpc-cni-c4","t":"Security groups for pods","p":"Normally all pods on a node share the node's security group. This feature lets a pod have its own security group through a dedicated branch ENI.","k":[["What problem does SecurityGroupPolicy solve?","It lets individual pods carry their own security group instead of sharing the node's."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s47-eks-vpc-cni-c5","t":"Network policy and service networking","p":"The VPC CNI can enforce Kubernetes NetworkPolicy using eBPF on supported versions. Service traffic goes through kube-proxy (iptables or IPVS).","k":[["What is the first policy to apply in a namespace?","A default-deny policy, followed by explicit allows."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s48-eks-identity-access","title":"EKS identity and access","c":[{"id":"s48-eks-identity-access-c1","t":"How authentication reaches Kubernetes","p":"kubectl on EKS signs a request with your IAM credentials (aws eks get-token). The EKS authenticator checks the signature, finds which Kubernetes identity your IAM principal maps to, and Kubernetes RBAC then decides what you can do.","k":[["Where do Kubernetes users come from on EKS?","From IAM principals mapped through access entries or aws-auth; Kubernetes itself has no user store."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s48-eks-identity-access-c2","t":"Access entries","p":"An access entry is an EKS API object that maps an IAM principal to Kubernetes permissions. Predefined access policies such as AmazonEKSClusterAdminPolicy or AmazonEKSViewPolicy can be scoped to namespaces.","k":[["Which authentication mode is recommended for new clusters?","API, which uses access entries only."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s48-eks-identity-access-c3","t":"The aws-auth ConfigMap (legacy)","p":"aws-auth in kube-system listed mapRoles and mapUsers entries. It still exists on older clusters and you will meet it while migrating.","k":[["Why migrate away from aws-auth?","It is error-prone, needs Kubernetes access to edit, and a typo can lock everyone out; access entries are API managed and audited."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s48-eks-identity-access-c4","t":"IRSA, IAM Roles for Service Accounts","p":"IRSA lets a Kubernetes service account assume an IAM role. The cluster's OIDC issuer signs a projected token; AWS STS trusts that issuer and swaps the token for temporary credentials.","k":[["Which claim restricts a role to one service account?","The token `sub` claim, formatted `system:serviceaccount:<ns>:<name>`."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s48-eks-identity-access-c5","t":"EKS Pod Identity","p":"Pod Identity is the newer, simpler mechanism. An agent on each node serves credentials to pods; you link a role to a service account with an EKS API call, not an OIDC trust policy per cluster.","k":[["What is the trust principal for Pod Identity roles?","The service principal pods.eks.amazonaws.com."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s48-eks-identity-access-c6","t":"IRSA versus Pod Identity and troubleshooting","p":"Both give pods temporary role credentials. Prefer Pod Identity on new EC2-based clusters, keep IRSA where Pod Identity is not supported (Fargate, some add-ons, other clusters).","k":[["How can a pod accidentally use the node role?","By falling back to IMDS when the SDK does not find IRSA or Pod Identity credentials."]],"o":[["Pod Identity","new EC2 or Karpenter based clusters, many clusters","the workload runs on Fargate"],["IRSA","Fargate, or a component that documents IRSA only","you want to avoid per-cluster OIDC trust"]]},{"id":"s48-eks-identity-access-c7","t":"Kubernetes RBAC on top of IAM","p":"Access entries map principals to groups; RBAC binds those groups to Roles and ClusterRoles. Keep roles narrow and namespaced.","k":[["Why bind roles to groups?","Membership then follows the IAM role, so removing access is one change in IAM."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s49-eks-addons","title":"EKS add-ons CoreDNS, kube-proxy and storage CSI","c":[{"id":"s49-eks-addons-c1","t":"What EKS add-ons are","p":"An add-on is a packaged operational component (VPC CNI, CoreDNS, kube-proxy, EBS CSI, Pod Identity Agent, metrics-server and more) that EKS installs and can update for you.","k":[["What does resolve-conflicts PRESERVE do?","It keeps fields you changed manually rather than overwriting them with the add-on defaults."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s49-eks-addons-c2","t":"CoreDNS","p":"CoreDNS answers every in-cluster DNS lookup, such as orders.shop.svc.cluster.local, and forwards other names to the VPC resolver.","k":[["Why does ndots:5 slow external lookups?","Names with fewer than five dots are tried with each search domain first, creating extra failing queries."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s49-eks-addons-c3","t":"kube-proxy","p":"kube-proxy programs each node so that a Service IP reaches one of its pods, using iptables rules or IPVS.","k":[["What changes in kube-proxy when a pod is replaced?","The endpoint list changes and rules on every node are updated to the new pod IP."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s49-eks-addons-c4","t":"EBS CSI driver","p":"The EBS CSI driver creates and attaches EBS volumes for PersistentVolumeClaims. In-tree volume plugins are removed, so every cluster needs this add-on for block storage.","k":[["Why use WaitForFirstConsumer?","The volume is then created in the AZ where the pod is scheduled instead of an arbitrary AZ."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s49-eks-addons-c5","t":"EFS CSI driver","p":"Amazon EFS is a shared NFS file system that works across AZs. The EFS CSI driver mounts it in pods, including on Fargate.","k":[["Which volume type supports ReadWriteMany across AZs?","EFS; an EBS volume attaches to one node in one AZ."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s49-eks-addons-c6","t":"Other useful add-ons","p":"A few more add-ons round out a production cluster: metrics-server, the Pod Identity Agent, the CloudWatch Observability add-on, and the CSI snapshot controller.","k":[["Which add-on provides data for the HPA?","metrics-server."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s50-eks-load-balancer-controller","title":"AWS Load Balancer Controller and ingress","c":[{"id":"s50-eks-load-balancer-controller-c1","t":"What the controller does","p":"The LBC watches Ingress and Service objects. For an Ingress with the alb class it builds an Application Load Balancer; for a Service of type LoadBalancer with the right annotation it builds a Network Load Balancer.","k":[["Which subnet tag selects internet-facing load balancer subnets?","kubernetes.io/role/elb=1."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s50-eks-load-balancer-controller-c2","t":"ALB Ingress","p":"An Ingress with ingressClassName: alb creates an ALB with listeners and rules from the Ingress paths and annotations.","k":[["Why prefer target-type ip?","The ALB talks straight to pods, avoiding the extra NodePort hop and giving even load distribution."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s50-eks-load-balancer-controller-c3","t":"NLB Services","p":"A Service of type LoadBalancer with the controller's annotations creates a Network Load Balancer for TCP, UDP or TLS traffic.","k":[["When would you pick an NLB over an ALB?","For non-HTTP protocols, static IPs, or very high throughput with low latency."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s50-eks-load-balancer-controller-c4","t":"Health checks, graceful shutdown and readiness gates","p":"A load balancer sends traffic only to targets marked healthy. Deployments can drop requests if pods leave the target group late or join too early.","k":[["What causes 502s during rolling updates?","The pod stops before the load balancer has deregistered it, so requests still reach a dead target."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s50-eks-load-balancer-controller-c5","t":"WAF, Shield, TLS and sharing ALBs","p":"Security features attach to the ALB through annotations: AWS WAF for request filtering, ACM for certificates, and Shield for DDoS.","k":[["Where does TLS terminate with an ALB?","At the ALB, using an ACM certificate; traffic to pods can be plain or re-encrypted."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s51-eks-upgrades-operations","title":"EKS upgrades and day-2 operations","c":[{"id":"s51-eks-upgrades-operations-c1","t":"Version support and cadence","p":"EKS supports each Kubernetes minor version for a standard support period, then offers a paid extended support period, after which the cluster is upgraded automatically.","k":[["Can you jump two minor versions at once?","No; upgrade one minor version at a time."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s51-eks-upgrades-operations-c2","t":"Upgrade order and procedure","p":"Upgrade the control plane first, then add-ons, then nodes. Each step is verified before the next.","k":[["Why must you not downgrade a control plane?","EKS does not support it; rollback means creating a new cluster, so test before upgrading production."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s51-eks-upgrades-operations-c3","t":"Blue/green cluster upgrades","p":"Instead of upgrading in place, build a second cluster on the new version, shift traffic to it, and retire the old one.","k":[],"o":[["In place","a good staging cluster, GitOps, PDBs in place","you cannot tolerate any rollback risk"],["Blue/green cluster","critical systems, big API changes","you lack automation to rebuild quickly"]]},{"id":"s51-eks-upgrades-operations-c4","t":"Disruption control with PDBs and topology spread","p":"A PodDisruptionBudget limits how many pods of an app may be voluntarily evicted at once. Topology spread constraints distribute pods across AZs and nodes.","k":[["What does a PDB with maxUnavailable 0 do to upgrades?","It blocks node drains, so the node update stalls."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s51-eks-upgrades-operations-c5","t":"Troubleshooting routine","p":"When nodes do not join, pods stay Pending or traffic fails, work from the control plane outward.","k":[["Where do you look first for a pod stuck in Pending?","The Events section of `kubectl describe pod`."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s52-ecr","title":"Amazon ECR registries and image supply chain","c":[{"id":"s52-ecr-c1","t":"Repositories and authentication","p":"An ECR registry exists per account and Region (111122223333.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com). Inside it you create one repository per image name.","k":[["Why use immutable tags?","A released tag always points at the same image, so deployments and audits are reproducible."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s52-ecr-c2","t":"Lifecycle policies","p":"A lifecycle policy deletes images automatically by age or count, based on rules you write.","k":[["Why preview a lifecycle policy?","A wrong rule can delete images that are still deployed or needed for rollback."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s52-ecr-c3","t":"Image scanning","p":"ECR checks images for known vulnerabilities (CVEs). Basic scanning uses the open source Clair-based database; enhanced scanning uses Amazon Inspector for continuous scanning of OS and language packages.","k":[["What does enhanced scanning add over basic?","Continuous rescans through Inspector, including language packages, with events and Security Hub integration."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s52-ecr-c4","t":"Replication and cross-Region DR","p":"ECR replicates images automatically to other Regions or accounts when they are pushed.","k":[["Are images pushed before the rule existed replicated?","No; only new pushes are replicated."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s52-ecr-c5","t":"Pull-through cache","p":"A pull-through cache rule makes ECR fetch and store images from an upstream registry (Docker Hub, Quay, GitHub Container Registry, registry.k8s.io, Amazon ECR Public, and others) on first pull.","k":[["Why use a cache for public images?","It avoids rate limits and outages of the upstream registry and lets you scan the images."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s52-ecr-c6","t":"Private access and permissions","p":"Nodes in private subnets reach ECR either through a NAT gateway or through VPC endpoints. Endpoints avoid NAT data processing charges and keep image traffic private.","k":[["Why is an S3 gateway endpoint part of private ECR access?","ECR serves image layers from S3, so layer downloads need an S3 path."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s53-karpenter-nodepools","title":"Karpenter provisioning and NodePools","c":[{"id":"s53-karpenter-nodepools-c1","t":"Karpenter versus Cluster Autoscaler","p":"The Cluster Autoscaler changes the size of pre-defined node groups. Karpenter has no node groups: it reads pod requirements and creates nodes of any suitable instance type.","k":[["Where should the Karpenter controller itself run?","On a small managed node group or Fargate, outside the capacity it provisions."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s53-karpenter-nodepools-c2","t":"EC2NodeClass","p":"An EC2NodeClass describes AWS-specific settings for nodes: AMI, subnets, security groups, IAM role, block devices and metadata options.","k":[["How does Karpenter find subnets?","By tag selectors in EC2NodeClass, usually `karpenter.sh/discovery`."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s53-karpenter-nodepools-c3","t":"NodePool requirements and limits","p":"A NodePool states which nodes Karpenter may create: instance categories, sizes, architectures, capacity types, zones and a total resource cap.","k":[["What does the limits field protect against?","A runaway workload or bug creating unbounded nodes and cost."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s53-karpenter-nodepools-c4","t":"Spot capacity and interruptions","p":"Spot instances cost much less but can be reclaimed with a two-minute notice. Karpenter listens for interruption events and replaces nodes proactively.","k":[["How much warning does a Spot interruption give?","Two minutes."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s53-karpenter-nodepools-c5","t":"Pod specs that help Karpenter","p":"Karpenter packs pods based on their requests. Accurate requests, labels and topology rules lead to efficient nodes.","k":[["What does Karpenter use to size nodes?","The resource requests of pending pods."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s54-karpenter-consolidation","title":"Karpenter consolidation, drift and operations","c":[{"id":"s54-karpenter-consolidation-c1","t":"Consolidation","p":"Consolidation deletes empty nodes and replaces underused nodes with fewer or cheaper ones, moving pods in between.","k":[["What is the difference between WhenEmpty and WhenEmptyOrUnderutilized?","The second also repacks partly used nodes, not only removes empty ones."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s54-karpenter-consolidation-c2","t":"Disruption budgets","p":"A budget limits how many nodes Karpenter may disrupt at once, optionally only in a time window and only for certain reasons.","k":[["Do budgets limit Spot interruptions?","No; involuntary disruptions are outside budgets."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s54-karpenter-consolidation-c3","t":"Drift and expiry","p":"Drift means a node no longer matches its NodePool or EC2NodeClass (new AMI, changed subnets, changed requirements). Karpenter replaces drifted nodes. expireAfter replaces nodes at a maximum age.","k":[["What triggers drift?","A change in NodePool or EC2NodeClass settings, including a new selected AMI, so existing nodes no longer match."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s54-karpenter-consolidation-c4","t":"Debugging provisioning problems","p":"When pods stay Pending, Karpenter logs say why no instance could be launched.","k":[["Where do you look when no NodeClaim is created?","The Karpenter logs and the pod's scheduling constraints; requirements may be impossible or limits reached."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s54-karpenter-consolidation-c5","t":"Cost and safety checklist","p":"Karpenter saves money mostly through right-sizing, Spot and consolidation. Keep a short list of controls.","k":[["Which setting guards against a runaway scale-out?","NodePool limits."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s55-externaldns","title":"ExternalDNS and Route 53 automation","c":[{"id":"s55-externaldns-c1","t":"What ExternalDNS does","p":"ExternalDNS is a controller that reads hostnames from Ingress, Service and Gateway resources and creates matching records in a DNS provider such as Route 53.","k":[["Why does ExternalDNS write TXT records?","They mark ownership so it only changes records it created."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s55-externaldns-c2","t":"IAM permissions","p":"ExternalDNS needs to list hosted zones and change record sets, and nothing else. Scope the permissions to the zones it manages.","k":[["Which action needs to be restricted to a zone ARN?","ChangeResourceRecordSets, because it modifies records."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s55-externaldns-c3","t":"Installing and configuring","p":"Install via the official Helm chart and set the provider, domain filters, policy and TXT owner ID.","k":[["Why must txtOwnerId be unique per cluster?","Otherwise one cluster may overwrite or delete records that another cluster owns."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s55-externaldns-c4","t":"Annotations, private zones and split horizon","p":"Annotations on a Service or Ingress add hostnames, TTLs and per-record settings. Public and private hosted zones can both exist for the same name.","k":[["How can one cluster serve both public and private names?","Run separate ExternalDNS instances filtered to public and private zones."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s55-externaldns-c5","t":"Weighted records for migrations","p":"ExternalDNS supports Route 53 routing policies through annotations, so you can send 10 percent of traffic to a new cluster.","k":[["What must differ between two weighted records of the same name?","The set identifier."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s55-externaldns-c6","t":"Troubleshooting ExternalDNS","p":"Most problems are IAM, filters or ownership conflicts, and the controller log shows which.","k":[["What first checks apply when a record is missing?","The Ingress must have an address, the domain must match filters, IAM must allow the change, and no other owner must hold the record."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s56-cert-manager","title":"cert-manager and TLS certificates","c":[{"id":"s56-cert-manager-c1","t":"ACM versus cert-manager","p":"AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) issues free public certificates that attach to ALB, NLB (TLS listener), CloudFront and API Gateway. You cannot export the private key. cert-manager creates certificates as Kubernetes Secrets that pods can mount.","k":[["Can you export the private key of an ACM public certificate?","No; it stays inside AWS-integrated services."]],"o":[["ACM","an ALB or CloudFront terminates TLS","the pod itself needs the private key"],["cert-manager with Let's Encrypt","in-cluster ingress or a webhook needs public trust","you cannot use DNS-01 or HTTP-01 at all"],["cert-manager with private CA","internal mTLS between services","clients cannot trust your private root"]]},{"id":"s56-cert-manager-c2","t":"Installing cert-manager","p":"cert-manager runs as three deployments (controller, webhook, cainjector) and adds CRDs: Issuer, ClusterIssuer, Certificate, CertificateRequest.","k":[["When does cert-manager renew by default?","At two thirds of the certificate lifetime."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s56-cert-manager-c3","t":"ACME DNS-01 with Route 53","p":"DNS-01 proves domain ownership by creating a TXT record. It works for wildcard names and for clusters not reachable from the internet.","k":[["Why use the staging ACME server first?","It has far higher rate limits, so configuration mistakes do not lock you out."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s56-cert-manager-c4","t":"Private CA for internal mTLS","p":"A private CA issues certificates your own services trust. cert-manager can run a self-signed root in the cluster or call AWS Private CA.","k":[["Why prefer short certificate lifetimes?","Compromised keys expire quickly and automation makes rotation cheap."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s56-cert-manager-c5","t":"Monitoring and troubleshooting","p":"Certificates fail quietly: a renewal error shows up only on expiry day. Watch cert-manager's status and metrics.","k":[["Which metric helps alert on expiry?","certmanager_certificate_expiration_timestamp_seconds."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s57-external-secrets","title":"External Secrets with AWS Secrets Manager","c":[{"id":"s57-external-secrets-c1","t":"The problem with Kubernetes Secrets","p":"A Secret object is stored in etcd, readable by anyone with get secret rights in the namespace, and often committed to Git in some encoded form.","k":[["Is base64 encryption?","No; it is an encoding anyone can reverse."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s57-external-secrets-c2","t":"ESO architecture","p":"ESO adds custom resources: a SecretStore (or cluster-wide ClusterSecretStore) says how to reach the provider, and an ExternalSecret says which remote keys to pull and what Secret to create.","k":[["What is the difference between SecretStore and ExternalSecret?","The store defines how to authenticate to the provider; the ExternalSecret defines which values to fetch and the Secret to create."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s57-external-secrets-c3","t":"IAM and ClusterSecretStore","p":"ESO's service account assumes an IAM role that can read only the secrets it should. The store references that service account.","k":[["Why scope the policy to a prefix?","A compromised operator then reads only the intended secrets, not the whole account."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s57-external-secrets-c4","t":"ExternalSecret resources","p":"An ExternalSecret names remote keys and the Kubernetes Secret to produce.","k":[["Why do environment variables not follow secret rotation automatically?","Environment values are set at container start; only mounted files update in place."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s57-external-secrets-c5","t":"Rotation and reload","p":"Secrets Manager can rotate credentials automatically with a Lambda. ESO then copies the new value, and applications must pick it up.","k":[["What triggers a pod restart after a Secret changes?","A tool such as Reloader that watches Secrets and performs a rollout."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s57-external-secrets-c6","t":"Alternatives and trade-offs","p":"ESO is one option. The AWS Secrets and Configuration Provider (ASCP) with the Secrets Store CSI driver mounts secrets directly into pods without creating a Kubernetes Secret.","k":[],"o":[["ESO","many apps need ordinary Secrets and env vars","secrets must never exist in etcd"],["CSI driver and ASCP","secrets should never be Kubernetes objects","you need env vars and cannot restart on change"],["SOPS","GitOps with a small number of static secrets","you need automatic rotation"]]}]},{"id":"s58-kms-secrets-manager","title":"KMS and Secrets Manager","c":[{"id":"s58-kms-secrets-manager-c1","t":"Envelope encryption","p":"KMS never encrypts your large data directly. It encrypts a small data key; the data key encrypts your data. You store the encrypted data key beside the data.","k":[["Why use data keys instead of sending data to KMS?","KMS limits request size and rate; a local data key encrypts bulk data quickly and KMS protects only the small key."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s58-kms-secrets-manager-c2","t":"Key types and rotation","p":"KMS keys come as AWS owned, AWS managed (aws/s3), or customer managed keys (CMKs). You get full control only with customer managed keys.","k":[["Does rotation change the key ID?","No; the key ID and ARN stay the same and old material is retained for decryption."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s58-kms-secrets-manager-c3","t":"Key policies and grants","p":"Every KMS key has a key policy, and it is the primary access control. IAM policies only work if the key policy allows IAM to control the key.","k":[["What does kms:ViaService do?","It restricts key use to requests made through the named AWS service."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s58-kms-secrets-manager-c4","t":"Secrets Manager basics","p":"Secrets Manager stores credentials, API keys and certificates as versioned secrets, encrypted with KMS, with fine-grained IAM and optional automatic rotation.","k":[["Which staging label marks the active version?","AWSCURRENT."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s58-kms-secrets-manager-c5","t":"Rotation with Lambda","p":"Rotation is a Lambda function that runs four steps: create a pending secret, set it in the database, test it, then finish by promoting it to current.","k":[["Which rotation strategy avoids downtime?","Alternating users, because one valid user always remains while the other changes."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s58-kms-secrets-manager-c6","t":"Parameter Store versus Secrets Manager","p":"Systems Manager Parameter Store holds configuration and, with SecureString, small secrets. It is cheaper but lacks built-in rotation.","k":[["When choose Secrets Manager over Parameter Store?","When you need automatic rotation, replication or cross-account sharing of credentials."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s59-iam-identity-center-governance","title":"IAM Identity Center, SCPs and access governance","c":[{"id":"s59-iam-identity-center-governance-c1","t":"IAM Identity Center","p":"IAM Identity Center (successor to AWS SSO) gives your workforce single sign-on to all accounts in an Organization. Users sign in once through your identity provider (Astra Connect, Okta, Entra ID) and pick an account and role.","k":[["What does Identity Center create in a member account?","An IAM role per assigned permission set, named AWSReservedSSO."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s59-iam-identity-center-governance-c2","t":"Permission sets and groups","p":"A permission set combines AWS managed policies, customer managed policies, an inline policy, a permissions boundary and a session duration.","k":[["Why assign permission sets to groups rather than users?","Joiners and leavers then change group membership in the IdP, not AWS configuration."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s59-iam-identity-center-governance-c3","t":"Service control policies","p":"An SCP is an Organization policy that sets the maximum permissions for accounts in an OU. It never grants access; it only limits what IAM can allow.","k":[["Does an SCP grant permissions?","No; it only restricts what IAM policies may allow."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s59-iam-identity-center-governance-c4","t":"Permission boundaries and least privilege","p":"A permissions boundary caps the permissions an IAM role can ever have, even if someone attaches AdministratorAccess to it.","k":[["What stops a developer from creating a role with admin rights?","The boundary condition forces the new role to carry a boundary that caps its permissions."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s59-iam-identity-center-governance-c5","t":"IAM Access Analyzer","p":"Access Analyzer finds resources shared outside your account or Organization, validates policies, and reports unused access.","k":[["What does external access analysis detect?","Resources shared outside your account or organisation, such as public buckets or cross-account trust."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s59-iam-identity-center-governance-c6","t":"Organizations, accounts and break-glass","p":"Multiple accounts are the strongest security boundary: separate workloads, environments and logging into different accounts under one Organization.","k":[["Why use several accounts?","Accounts limit blast radius, quotas and permissions between workloads and environments."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s60-detection-guardduty-securityhub","title":"GuardDuty, Security Hub and Inspector","c":[{"id":"s60-detection-guardduty-securityhub-c1","t":"GuardDuty","p":"GuardDuty analyses CloudTrail events, VPC flow logs and DNS logs with threat intelligence and machine learning, and reports suspicious behaviour as findings.","k":[["Does GuardDuty need agents on instances for its core detection?","No; it reads CloudTrail, flow and DNS logs directly."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s60-detection-guardduty-securityhub-c2","t":"GuardDuty for EKS","p":"Two protections cover Kubernetes: EKS audit log monitoring (analyses the API audit stream) and runtime monitoring (an agent add-on watching processes, files and network inside nodes).","k":[["Which GuardDuty feature sees a reverse shell inside a container?","EKS runtime monitoring."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s60-detection-guardduty-securityhub-c3","t":"Security Hub","p":"Security Hub collects findings from GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie, Config, IAM Access Analyzer and partners into one format and one dashboard, and runs compliance checks against standards.","k":[["What is ASFF?","The AWS Security Finding Format, a common schema for findings from different tools."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s60-detection-guardduty-securityhub-c4","t":"Amazon Inspector","p":"Inspector scans EC2 instances, ECR container images and Lambda functions for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure.","k":[["What makes Inspector scanning continuous?","It re-evaluates resources when new vulnerabilities are published, not only at scan time."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s60-detection-guardduty-securityhub-c5","t":"Automated response with EventBridge","p":"EventBridge rules match findings and trigger Lambda, SSM Automation, Step Functions or notifications.","k":[["How can you test automation safely?","Generate GuardDuty sample findings and watch the rule fire."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s61-config-cloudtrail","title":"CloudTrail and AWS Config","c":[{"id":"s61-config-cloudtrail-c1","t":"CloudTrail events and trails","p":"CloudTrail logs every AWS API call (console, CLI, SDK, other services) with the caller, time, source IP, request and response outcome.","k":[["How long is CloudTrail event history kept without a trail?","90 days for management events."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s61-config-cloudtrail-c2","t":"Protecting and querying logs","p":"Logs are evidence. Make them immutable and searchable.","k":[["Which control stops an administrator from turning logging off?","An SCP denying StopLogging and DeleteTrail, plus Object Lock on the bucket."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s61-config-cloudtrail-c3","t":"Reading a CloudTrail event","p":"Each event is a JSON record. A few fields answer most questions.","k":[["What does sessionIssuer tell you?","Which IAM role an assumed-role session came from."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s61-config-cloudtrail-c4","t":"AWS Config recorder and history","p":"Config records the configuration of supported resources and every change, building a timeline per resource.","k":[["What does Config give that CloudTrail does not?","The full configuration of a resource over time, not just the API call."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s61-config-cloudtrail-c5","t":"Config rules, conformance packs and remediation","p":"A Config rule evaluates resources as compliant or noncompliant. A conformance pack bundles many rules. Remediation actions fix findings with SSM Automation.","k":[["How does a custom Guard rule differ from a managed rule?","You write the evaluation logic as policy-as-code, while managed rules are prebuilt."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s61-config-cloudtrail-c6","t":"Investigation workflow","p":"Combine the tools: Config shows what changed, CloudTrail shows who changed it, GuardDuty shows whether it looked malicious.","k":[["In which order do you use Config and CloudTrail?","Config pinpoints what and when; CloudTrail then identifies who and from where."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s62-waf-shield-macie","title":"WAF, Shield, Macie and edge protection","c":[{"id":"s62-waf-shield-macie-c1","t":"AWS WAF web ACLs","p":"WAF inspects HTTP requests at an ALB, CloudFront, API Gateway or AppSync and allows, blocks, counts or challenges them based on rules.","k":[["Why start a new rule in Count mode?","To see what it would block in the logs before risking blocking real users."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s62-waf-shield-macie-c2","t":"WAF logging and tuning","p":"WAF logs every inspected request, including which rule matched, to CloudWatch Logs, S3 or Firehose.","k":[["What lets you keep a managed rule group but allow one path?","A rule exclusion or a scope-down statement."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s62-waf-shield-macie-c3","t":"AWS Shield","p":"Shield protects against DDoS attacks. Standard is automatic and free on all AWS resources; Advanced adds detection tuning, the response team, and cost protection.","k":[["What does Shield Advanced add to Standard?","Tuned detection, access to the response team, and cost protection for attack-driven scaling."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s62-waf-shield-macie-c4","t":"Network Firewall and security groups","p":"Security groups filter by IP and port at the instance or pod level. AWS Network Firewall filters at the VPC edge with domain and signature rules.","k":[["Why is egress filtering valuable?","Compromised workloads need outbound access to download tools or exfiltrate data; blocking it limits damage."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s62-waf-shield-macie-c5","t":"Amazon Macie","p":"Macie scans S3 buckets for sensitive data (personal information, credentials, financial data) using managed and custom identifiers, and reports on bucket security posture.","k":[["What does Macie examine?","The contents and configuration of S3 buckets to find sensitive data and risky settings."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s62-waf-shield-macie-c6","t":"Data protection checklist","p":"A short list of controls that apply to every data store on a typical EKS application.","k":[["Which condition key forces TLS in S3 policies?","aws:SecureTransport."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s63-cloudwatch-metrics-alarms","title":"CloudWatch metrics and alarms","c":[{"id":"s63-cloudwatch-metrics-alarms-c1","t":"Metrics, namespaces and dimensions","p":"A metric is a time series identified by a namespace (AWS/RDS), a name (CPUUtilization) and dimensions (key-value labels such as DBInstanceIdentifier=shop-db).","k":[["Why are two metrics with different dimensions distinct series?","Each dimension combination defines its own time series."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s63-cloudwatch-metrics-alarms-c2","t":"Alarms","p":"An alarm watches one metric (or a math expression) and changes state between OK, ALARM and INSUFFICIENTDATA based on a threshold over several periods.","k":[["What does 3 datapoints out of 5 mean?","The alarm fires when at least 3 of the last 5 periods breach the threshold."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s63-cloudwatch-metrics-alarms-c3","t":"Metric math, anomaly detection and composite alarms","p":"Metric math computes new series, such as an error rate, from other metrics. Anomaly detection learns a normal band. Composite alarms combine alarms with AND/OR.","k":[["Why prefer a rate alarm over a raw count?","A rate stays meaningful as traffic changes, avoiding false alarms at peaks and missed ones at quiet times."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s63-cloudwatch-metrics-alarms-c4","t":"Custom metrics and the embedded metric format","p":"Applications can publish their own metrics with PutMetricData or by writing JSON logs in the Embedded Metric Format (EMF), which CloudWatch turns into metrics.","k":[["Why avoid user IDs as dimensions?","Each unique dimension value creates a new billable metric, causing huge cost and unusable dashboards."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s63-cloudwatch-metrics-alarms-c5","t":"Dashboards and notification routing","p":"Dashboards show golden signals across services; SNS and AWS Chatbot route alarms to people.","k":[["What should page a human?","Customer-impacting symptoms such as error rate or latency SLO breaches, not every resource warning."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s64-cloudwatch-logs-insights","title":"CloudWatch Logs, Logs Insights and Container Insights","c":[{"id":"s64-cloudwatch-logs-insights-c1","t":"Log groups, streams and retention","p":"Logs are stored in log groups (one per application or source) that contain log streams (one per source instance). Retention and encryption are set on the group.","k":[["What is the usual hidden cost of CloudWatch Logs?","Log groups with no retention accumulate storage forever, and high-volume ingestion is billed per GB."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s64-cloudwatch-logs-insights-c2","t":"Shipping container logs from EKS","p":"Pods write to stdout. A DaemonSet agent (Fluent Bit, or the CloudWatch agent from the Observability add-on) reads node log files and ships them with Kubernetes metadata.","k":[["Why log JSON?","Logs Insights and other tools can then filter by fields without brittle text parsing."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s64-cloudwatch-logs-insights-c3","t":"Logs Insights queries","p":"Logs Insights is an interactive query language for log groups: filter, parse, aggregate, sort.","k":[["What drives Logs Insights cost?","The amount of log data scanned by each query."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s64-cloudwatch-logs-insights-c4","t":"Metric filters and subscription filters","p":"A metric filter turns matching log lines into a CloudWatch metric. A subscription filter streams logs in near real time to another service.","k":[["Do metric filters process historical logs?","No, only events ingested after the filter exists."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s64-cloudwatch-logs-insights-c5","t":"Container Insights","p":"Container Insights collects cluster, node, pod and container metrics (CPU, memory, network, restarts) and presents them in prebuilt dashboards, with optional enhanced observability for EKS.","k":[["Why can Container Insights get expensive?","Metrics are produced per pod and per container, so churn and cardinality increase the metric count."]],"o":[["Container Insights","quick start, AWS native alarms","very high pod churn and cardinality"],["Prometheus with AMP","PromQL, SLOs, portable across clouds","you want zero setup"],["OpenTelemetry (ADOT)","traces and metrics from apps","you only need node metrics"]]}]},{"id":"s65-well-architected-overview","title":"Well-Architected framework and the three-tier shop","c":[{"id":"s65-well-architected-overview-c1","t":"The six pillars","p":"Six pillars group the questions: Operational Excellence, Security, Reliability, Performance Efficiency, Cost Optimization and Sustainability.","k":[["Is Well-Architected a pass or fail audit?","No; it is a review that identifies and prioritises risks and trade-offs."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s65-well-architected-overview-c2","t":"How a review works","p":"You pick a workload, answer the lens questions with your team, see high and medium risk items, and create an improvement plan.","k":[["How often should you repeat a review?","At least yearly and after major architecture changes."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s65-well-architected-overview-c3","t":"The reference application","p":"Throughout this section the example is a shop with a web tier, an API tier and a data tier, running on EKS in a three-AZ VPC.","k":[["Where do database instances live?","In isolated database subnets with no route to the internet."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s65-well-architected-overview-c4","t":"Operational excellence applied","p":"Make every change small, automated, observable and reversible.","k":[["Why prefer pull requests for infrastructure changes?","They give review, history and the ability to revert a known state."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s65-well-architected-overview-c5","t":"Security applied","p":"Apply least privilege and defence in depth at each tier.","k":[["Which Kubernetes mechanism enforces non-root, no privileged pods per namespace?","Pod Security Admission, with the restricted profile."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s66-well-architected-reliability-performance","title":"Reliability, performance, cost and sustainability in the shop","c":[{"id":"s66-well-architected-reliability-performance-c1","t":"Failure domains and static stability","p":"A failure domain is the largest thing that fails together: a pod, a node, an AZ, a Region, an account. Static stability means the system keeps working during a failure without needing new capacity or manual action.","k":[["What does static stability mean?","The workload continues to operate during a failure without launching new capacity or manual intervention."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s66-well-architected-reliability-performance-c2","t":"Health, retries and graceful degradation","p":"Probes tell Kubernetes what is healthy; timeouts, retries with backoff and circuit breakers stop one failure from spreading.","k":[["Why not check the database in a liveness probe?","A database outage would restart every pod and make the incident worse."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s66-well-architected-reliability-performance-c3","t":"Scaling the tiers","p":"Scale pods with HPA (or KEDA for event signals), nodes with Karpenter, and data with replicas, caching and capacity settings.","k":[["What gives headroom while Karpenter launches nodes?","Low-priority pause pods that are evicted when real pods need the space."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s66-well-architected-reliability-performance-c4","t":"Performance efficiency","p":"Choose the right resource type, measure, and keep reviewing as AWS releases new options.","k":[["Why track p99 latency rather than the average?","Averages hide the slow tail that real users experience."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s66-well-architected-reliability-performance-c5","t":"Cost and sustainability levers","p":"Cost and sustainability mostly push the same way: use less, and use it efficiently.","k":[["Name one change that cuts both cost and energy use.","Right-sizing or removing idle capacity."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s66-well-architected-reliability-performance-c6","t":"Pillar trade-offs and a review checklist","p":"Pillars compete: more redundancy raises cost, more controls slow releases. Decide per workload and write it down.","k":[],"o":[["Warm standby","revenue loss per minute is high","the app tolerates hours of downtime"],["Backup and restore","the system is internal or rebuilt easily","RTO is under an hour"]]}]},{"id":"s67-multi-az-design","title":"Multi-AZ design for the three-tier shop","c":[{"id":"s67-multi-az-design-c1","t":"What an AZ gives you","p":"An AZ is one or more data centres with independent power, cooling and networking, connected to others in the Region by fast links.","k":[["Why use AZ IDs in cross-account work?","AZ names map to different physical zones in different accounts, but IDs are consistent."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s67-multi-az-design-c2","t":"Network layout across AZs","p":"Replicate each subnet tier in every AZ, give each AZ its own NAT Gateway, and route private subnets to the NAT in the same AZ.","k":[["Why one NAT Gateway per AZ?","It avoids a single point of failure and cross-AZ data processing for egress."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s67-multi-az-design-c3","t":"Compute and pod placement","p":"Spread nodes and pods across all AZs, keep spare capacity in each, and make volumes zone-aware.","k":[["Can an EBS volume follow a pod to another AZ?","No; an EBS volume is bound to one AZ."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s67-multi-az-design-c4","t":"Data tier failure behaviour","p":"Each data service handles AZ loss differently; know the recovery time and client behaviour of each.","k":[["Why do clients matter during failover?","DNS caching, connection pools and retry logic decide how fast the application actually recovers."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s67-multi-az-design-c5","t":"Cross-AZ traffic costs and balance","p":"Traffic between AZs is billed per GB in each direction. Multi-AZ designs must watch chatty paths.","k":[["Name two sources of hidden cross-AZ traffic.","Service calls spread across zones, and cross-zone load balancing or replication."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s67-multi-az-design-c6","t":"Fault injection and game days","p":"Prove the design works by breaking it on purpose in a controlled way.","k":[["What is a stop condition in FIS?","An alarm that aborts the experiment if customer impact appears."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s68-disaster-recovery","title":"Disaster recovery patterns","c":[{"id":"s68-disaster-recovery-c1","t":"RTO, RPO and the DR spectrum","p":"RPO (recovery point objective) is how much data you can lose, measured in time. RTO (recovery time objective) is how long recovery may take. Lower targets cost more.","k":[["What does RPO measure?","The maximum acceptable amount of data loss, expressed as time."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s68-disaster-recovery-c2","t":"Backup and restore","p":"Take regular backups, copy them to another Region and account, and rebuild the environment from infrastructure as code when needed.","k":[["Why copy backups to another account as well as another Region?","It protects against account compromise and deletion, not only a Region outage."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s68-disaster-recovery-c3","t":"Pilot light","p":"Keep the data replicated and the core infrastructure defined in the DR Region, but keep compute switched off or at minimum size. On disaster, scale up.","k":[["What stays off in a pilot light?","Most compute; data replication and core definitions stay on."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s68-disaster-recovery-c4","t":"Warm standby and active-active","p":"Warm standby runs a smaller but complete copy that can serve traffic at once and be scaled up. Active-active serves users from both Regions simultaneously.","k":[["What is the hardest part of active-active?","Data consistency across Regions, including write conflicts."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s68-disaster-recovery-c5","t":"DR for the EKS control plane and cluster state","p":"A second cluster is rebuilt from code. Application state lives in databases and object stores, not in the cluster.","k":[["Why not rely on backing up etcd for DR?","EKS does not expose etcd; rebuild clusters from code and keep state in data stores."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s68-disaster-recovery-c6","t":"Runbooks, rehearsals and failback","p":"A DR plan that was never exercised does not work. Write a runbook, run it, and record the actual times.","k":[["Why is failback often harder than failover?","Data has changed in the DR Region and must be synchronised back before traffic can return safely."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s69-cost-visibility","title":"Cost visibility with Cost Explorer, CUR and tagging","c":[{"id":"s69-cost-visibility-c1","t":"Billing basics and cost types","p":"AWS bills monthly per payer account, with usage priced by unit (instance-hour, GB-month, request). Different cost types answer different questions.","k":[["Which cost type suits team showback of Savings Plans?","Amortized (or net amortized), because upfront fees are spread across the term."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s69-cost-visibility-c2","t":"Cost Explorer","p":"Cost Explorer is the console and API to explore costs and usage over time, filtered and grouped by service, account, Region, usage type, tag and more.","k":[["Which grouping reveals NAT gateway data charges?","Usage type, such as NatGateway-Bytes."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s69-cost-visibility-c3","t":"Tagging strategy and cost allocation tags","p":"Tags are key-value labels on resources. Cost allocation tags must be activated in the Billing console before they appear in cost reports, and they apply from activation forward.","k":[["Do cost allocation tags apply to past costs?","No, only to usage after they are activated (backfill is a separate request)."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s69-cost-visibility-c4","t":"Cost and Usage Report and Data Exports","p":"The Cost and Usage Report (CUR) is the most detailed dataset: one row per resource per line item per hour or day, delivered to S3 as Parquet or CSV. Data Exports is the newer interface that also offers CUR 2.0 and FOCUS formats.","k":[["What does CUR give that Cost Explorer does not?","Line-item detail per resource and tag that you can join and query with SQL."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s69-cost-visibility-c5","t":"Kubernetes cost allocation","p":"EKS bills for nodes and volumes, not pods. To attribute spend to teams you need to divide node cost by pod resource use.","k":[["Why can EC2 cost not be assigned to a team directly in a shared cluster?","Nodes run pods from many teams, so cost must be divided by pod resource use."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s70-budgets-anomalies-governance","title":"Budgets, anomaly detection and cost governance","c":[{"id":"s70-budgets-anomalies-governance-c1","t":"AWS Budgets","p":"A budget tracks cost, usage, Savings Plans coverage or utilisation against a target and sends alerts when actual or forecast spend crosses thresholds.","k":[["What is the benefit of a forecasted alert?","It warns before the limit is actually reached."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s70-budgets-anomalies-governance-c2","t":"Cost Anomaly Detection","p":"Cost Anomaly Detection uses machine learning to flag unexpected spend by service, account, tag or cost category and ranks the likely root cause.","k":[["What does a monitor define?","The scope of spend the anomaly detection model watches."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s70-budgets-anomalies-governance-c3","t":"Guardrails against expensive mistakes","p":"Prevention beats detection. Limit what can be created, where and how big.","k":[["Why use preventive controls as well as budgets?","Budgets alert after spend occurs; guardrails stop the expensive action first."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s70-budgets-anomalies-governance-c4","t":"A monthly FinOps routine","p":"FinOps is a habit: inform, optimise, operate. A short monthly review keeps spend aligned with value.","k":[["What is a useful unit cost metric?","Cost per order, request or tenant, which ties spend to business value."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s71-savings-plans-reserved","title":"Savings Plans, Reserved Instances and commitment strategy","c":[{"id":"s71-savings-plans-reserved-c1","t":"Pricing models at a glance","p":"You can pay on-demand, commit to a steady spend, bid for spare capacity (Spot), or reserve specific capacity.","k":[["What is the usual order: commit or rightsize first?","Rightsize first, then commit to the stable baseline."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s71-savings-plans-reserved-c2","t":"Savings Plan types","p":"Compute Savings Plans apply across EC2 families, Regions, OS, tenancy, Fargate and Lambda. EC2 Instance Savings Plans give a deeper discount for a chosen family in a Region. SageMaker and database plans also exist.","k":[["Which plan type suits Karpenter with changing instance types?","Compute Savings Plans."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s71-savings-plans-reserved-c3","t":"Reserved Instances and what is left of them","p":"RIs reserve a specific instance type, platform and scope (Regional or zonal) for 1 or 3 years. Standard RIs give the best discount; Convertible RIs can be exchanged.","k":[["Which services still rely on reserved nodes rather than Savings Plans?","Typically RDS, ElastiCache and OpenSearch (check current coverage)."]],"o":[["Compute Savings Plan","EKS and EC2 with changing families, Fargate","you need guaranteed capacity in a zone"],["EC2 Instance Savings Plan","stable family and Region","you plan to migrate families soon"],["Reserved nodes for RDS or ElastiCache","stable database sizes","you may move to Aurora Serverless"],["Capacity Reservation","scheduled events needing capacity","you want a discount alone"]]},{"id":"s71-savings-plans-reserved-c4","t":"Sizing a commitment","p":"Commit to the level of usage you are certain to have, not the average and certainly not the peak.","k":[["What number should a first commitment be sized from?","The stable minimum hourly usage after rightsizing, not the average or the peak."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s71-savings-plans-reserved-c5","t":"Monitoring and avoiding mistakes","p":"Commitments need ongoing care: watch utilisation (are we using what we bought) and coverage (how much eligible usage is covered).","k":[["What does low utilisation mean?","You are paying for commitment you are not using."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s72-rightsizing-spot","title":"Rightsizing, Spot and efficient compute","c":[{"id":"s72-rightsizing-spot-c1","t":"Rightsizing EC2 and databases","p":"Rightsizing matches instance size to measured usage. Many instances run at under 20 percent CPU.","k":[["Why install the CloudWatch agent for rightsizing?","EC2 does not report memory by default, so recommendations without it ignore memory use."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s72-rightsizing-spot-c2","t":"Rightsizing Kubernetes requests","p":"Pod requests drive node count. Inflated requests waste nodes; low requests cause throttling and eviction.","k":[["What does updateMode Off do?","It computes recommendations without changing running pods."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s72-rightsizing-spot-c3","t":"Spot instances","p":"Spot instances use spare EC2 capacity at a large discount, but AWS can reclaim them with two minutes' notice.","k":[["How many minutes of notice does a Spot interruption give?","Two minutes."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s72-rightsizing-spot-c4","t":"Graviton, storage and other unit-cost levers","p":"Cheaper hardware and better storage choices lower cost per unit of work without reducing capacity.","k":[["Why build multi-architecture images?","They let the same image run on cheaper Graviton nodes as well as x86."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s72-rightsizing-spot-c5","t":"Scheduling off-hours and scaling to zero","p":"Development and test environments rarely need to run at night or on weekends.","k":[["What cost remains when a dev cluster is scaled to zero nodes?","The EKS control plane fee, storage and other fixed resources."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s73-data-transfer-networking-costs","title":"Data transfer and networking costs","c":[{"id":"s73-data-transfer-networking-costs-c1","t":"The pricing rules","p":"Roughly: data in from the internet is free, data out to the internet is charged per GB, traffic between AZs in a Region is charged per GB, and traffic inside one AZ over private IPs is free.","k":[["Is traffic between two instances in the same AZ always free?","Only over private IPs; using public or Elastic IPs is charged."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s73-data-transfer-networking-costs-c2","t":"NAT Gateway costs","p":"A NAT Gateway charges an hourly fee plus a per-GB data processing charge on everything that passes through, in addition to normal data transfer.","k":[["Which endpoint type is free?","Gateway endpoints for S3 and DynamoDB."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s73-data-transfer-networking-costs-c3","t":"Internet egress and CloudFront","p":"Serving users directly from EC2 or ALB incurs internet egress at the standard rate. CloudFront often costs less per GB and reduces origin load.","k":[["Is transfer from an S3 origin to CloudFront charged?","No, origin-to-CloudFront transfer from AWS origins is free; CloudFront to users is billed."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s73-data-transfer-networking-costs-c4","t":"Cross-AZ and cross-Region traffic in practice","p":"Distributed systems send data between zones constantly: service calls, replication, consumers, backups.","k":[["What is one way to keep Kubernetes service traffic in-zone?","Topology Aware Routing on the Service."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s73-data-transfer-networking-costs-c5","t":"A checklist for transfer savings","p":"Work through the big levers in order of effort versus payoff.","k":[["Which saving is free and immediate?","Adding S3 and DynamoDB gateway endpoints."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s74-cloudformation","title":"CloudFormation stacks, change sets and drift","c":[{"id":"s74-cloudformation-c1","t":"Templates and stacks","p":"A template (YAML or JSON) declares resources. A stack is the deployed instance of a template, managed as one unit.","k":[["What happens if you rename a resource's logical ID?","CloudFormation creates a new resource and deletes the old one."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s74-cloudformation-c2","t":"Intrinsic functions and pseudo parameters","p":"Intrinsic functions such as !Ref, !GetAtt, !Sub, !Join, !Select and !ImportValue compute values at deploy time.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s74-cloudformation-c3","t":"Change sets and stack policies","p":"A change set shows exactly what an update will add, modify, replace or remove before you apply it. A stack policy blocks updates to protected resources.","k":[["Which column warns that an update recreates a resource?","Replacement set to True."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s74-cloudformation-c4","t":"Drift detection","p":"Drift is when real resources differ from what the stack template says, usually because someone changed them by hand.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s74-cloudformation-c5","t":"Nested stacks, exports and StackSets","p":"Nested stacks compose templates. StackSets deploy one template to many accounts and Regions.","k":[["Why prefer SSM parameters over exports for sharing?","Exports lock the producing stack; parameters decouple lifecycles."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s74-cloudformation-c6","t":"Custom resources, macros and hooks","p":"Custom resources run your Lambda during stack operations. Macros transform templates. Hooks validate resources before provisioning.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s74-cloudformation-c7","t":"Troubleshooting stack failures","p":"Most failures are permissions, quotas, name conflicts, dependency order or a rollback loop.","k":[["Which command resumes a stuck UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED stack?","continue-update-rollback."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s75-cdk","title":"AWS CDK constructs and pipelines","c":[{"id":"s75-cdk-c1","t":"What CDK is","p":"CDK is a framework where you write code that creates objects called constructs. Running cdk synth turns them into CloudFormation templates, and cdk deploy applies them.","k":[["What does cdk synth produce?","CloudFormation templates (a cloud assembly)."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s75-cdk-c2","t":"Construct levels L1, L2 and L3","p":"L1 constructs map one to one to CloudFormation resources (CfnBucket). L2 add sensible defaults and helper methods. L3 (patterns) combine several resources into a solution.","k":[["Which level should you reach for first?","L2, dropping to L1 only when a feature is missing."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s75-cdk-c3","t":"Bootstrapping and environments","p":"Before first deploy, cdk bootstrap creates a CDKToolkit stack in each account and Region with an assets bucket, ECR repository and roles.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s75-cdk-c4","t":"Diff, deploy and context","p":"cdk diff compares your code against the deployed stack. Context caches lookups such as VPC IDs in cdk.context.json.","k":[["Why commit cdk.context.json?","Lookups stay stable so builds are repeatable."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s75-cdk-c5","t":"Testing and aspects","p":"You can unit test synthesized templates, and use Aspects to enforce rules across all constructs.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s75-cdk-c6","t":"CDK Pipelines","p":"CDK Pipelines creates a self-mutating CodePipeline that builds, tests and deploys your CDK app across stages and accounts.","k":[["What does the self mutate stage do?","Updates the pipeline itself when its definition in code changes."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s75-cdk-c7","t":"Sharing constructs and CDK vs Terraform","p":"Publish well-tested constructs as packages so teams reuse your standards. Choose CDK or Terraform by team skills and estate.","k":[],"o":[["CDK","AWS-centric dev teams who like code","you must manage many non-AWS providers"],["Terraform","multi-cloud or platform team standard","your team wants L3 patterns with generated IAM"],["CloudFormation","you use SAM, StackSets or Service Catalog","you need loops and abstractions"]]}]},{"id":"s76-codebuild-codepipeline","title":"CodeBuild and CodePipeline","c":[{"id":"s76-codebuild-codepipeline-c1","t":"CodeBuild projects","p":"A CodeBuild project runs commands from a buildspec in a fresh container and bills by the build minute.","k":[["How is CodeBuild billed?","By build minutes at the chosen compute size."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s76-codebuild-codepipeline-c2","t":"The buildspec","p":"buildspec.yml defines phases, environment variables, artifacts and cache.","k":[["Which phase still runs after a failed build phase?","post_build."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s76-codebuild-codepipeline-c3","t":"Building and pushing container images","p":"CodeBuild commonly builds a Docker image and pushes it to ECR with an immutable tag.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s76-codebuild-codepipeline-c4","t":"CodePipeline stages and actions","p":"A pipeline is a series of stages, each with actions (source, build, test, approval, deploy, invoke) that pass artifacts forward.","k":[["How are artifacts passed between stages?","Through an encrypted S3 artifact store."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s76-codebuild-codepipeline-c5","t":"Cross-account and cross-Region deployment","p":"Pipelines usually live in a tooling account and deploy into workload accounts by assuming roles there.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s76-codebuild-codepipeline-c6","t":"Triggers, approvals and notifications","p":"Pipelines start on source changes and can pause for approval, with events sent to chat or email.","k":[["Who can approve a manual approval action?","Principals whose IAM policy allows codepipeline:PutApprovalResult on it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s76-codebuild-codepipeline-c7","t":"Troubleshooting builds and pipelines","p":"Failures cluster around permissions, missing artifacts, network access and buildspec errors.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s77-codedeploy","title":"CodeDeploy deployment strategies","c":[{"id":"s77-codedeploy-c1","t":"What CodeDeploy does","p":"CodeDeploy coordinates releasing a new revision to a fleet, a Lambda alias or an ECS service, following an appspec file and a deployment configuration.","k":[["Which platforms does CodeDeploy support?","EC2 or on-premises, Lambda and ECS."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s77-codedeploy-c2","t":"In-place and rolling deployments on EC2","p":"An in-place deployment stops the app on each instance, installs the new revision and restarts it, in batches.","k":[["10 instances, HalfAtATime. Capacity during a batch?","50 percent (5 of 10 serving)."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s77-codedeploy-c3","t":"Blue/green deployments","p":"Blue/green creates a complete new environment (green), tests it, then moves traffic from blue and keeps blue for quick rollback.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s77-codedeploy-c4","t":"Canary and linear traffic shifting","p":"Canary sends a small percentage first, then the rest. Linear adds a fixed percentage on an interval.","k":[["How long does Linear10PercentEvery1Minute take to reach 100 percent?","About 10 minutes (ten steps of one minute), the last step completing the shift."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s77-codedeploy-c5","t":"Alarms and automatic rollback","p":"CodeDeploy can watch CloudWatch alarms and automatically roll back if they fire.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s77-codedeploy-c6","t":"Lifecycle hooks and agent","p":"Hooks run scripts or Lambda functions at defined points; EC2 deployments need the CodeDeploy agent installed.","k":[["What must a Lambda hook call back to CodeDeploy?","PutLifecycleEventHookExecutionStatus with Succeeded or Failed."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s77-codedeploy-c7","t":"Choosing a deployment strategy","p":"Pick by risk, cost of duplicate capacity and how quickly you must roll back.","k":[],"o":[["In-place","dev and low risk with spare capacity","strict uptime requirements"],["Rolling","stateless fleets with health checks","schema changes incompatible with old version"],["Blue/green canary","production APIs with alarms","cost of duplicate capacity is prohibited"]]}]},{"id":"s78-systems-manager","title":"Systems Manager operations toolkit","c":[{"id":"s78-systems-manager-c1","t":"Managed nodes and the SSM Agent","p":"A managed node is a machine running the SSM Agent whose IAM role lets it talk to Systems Manager.","k":[["An instance does not appear in Fleet Manager. Name three causes.","Missing role permissions, no network path to the SSM endpoints, or the agent not running."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s78-systems-manager-c2","t":"Session Manager","p":"Session Manager gives a shell on an instance through the AWS API, with no inbound ports, bastion hosts or key pairs.","k":[["How do you restrict engineers to their own team's instances?","An IAM condition on ssm:resourceTag for StartSession."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s78-systems-manager-c3","t":"Parameter Store","p":"Parameter Store is a hierarchical key value store for configuration and secrets, with versions and IAM access.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s78-systems-manager-c4","t":"Run Command and State Manager","p":"Run Command runs a document (script) on many nodes at once. State Manager keeps nodes in a desired state by applying documents on a schedule.","k":[["Which two settings limit the blast radius of Run Command?","max-concurrency and max-errors."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s78-systems-manager-c5","t":"Patch Manager","p":"Patch Manager scans nodes for missing OS patches and installs them in maintenance windows, using patch baselines.","k":[["What does approve_after_days do?","Waits that many days after a patch is released before the baseline approves it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s78-systems-manager-c6","t":"Automation runbooks","p":"Automation runs multi-step runbooks that call AWS APIs, with approvals, branching and rate control.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s78-systems-manager-c7","t":"Maintenance windows, Inventory and Change Manager","p":"Maintenance windows schedule tasks. Inventory lists software. Change Manager adds approval workflows.","k":[["Window duration 3 h and cutoff 1 h. When does the last task start?","At most 2 hours after the window starts."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s79-backup-drs","title":"AWS Backup and Elastic Disaster Recovery","c":[{"id":"s79-backup-drs-c1","t":"AWS Backup plans and vaults","p":"AWS Backup is one service to schedule, retain and monitor backups for EBS, RDS, Aurora, DynamoDB, EFS, FSx, S3, EC2 and more.","k":[["How do new resources get included automatically?","A tag based selection in the backup plan."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s79-backup-drs-c2","t":"Cross-account copies and Vault Lock","p":"Copy recovery points to another account and Region, and lock the vault so even administrators cannot delete them early.","k":[["What is the benefit of a separate backup account?","A compromise of a workload account cannot delete its own backups."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s79-backup-drs-c3","t":"RPO, RTO and restore time","p":"RPO is how much data you can lose, measured in time. RTO is how long you can be down. They drive the design and its cost.","k":[["Daily backups give what worst case RPO?","Up to 24 hours."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s79-backup-drs-c4","t":"DR strategies","p":"Four classic strategies trade cost against recovery speed: backup and restore, pilot light, warm standby and multi-site active-active.","k":[["Which strategy keeps a scaled-down full environment running?","Warm standby."]],"o":[["Backup and restore","internal systems with hours of tolerance","revenue systems with minutes of tolerance"],["Pilot light","important systems","strict minutes-level RTO"],["Warm standby","customer facing with minutes of tolerance","budget cannot carry a second stack"],["Active-active","near zero downtime is a requirement","data model cannot handle multi-writer conflicts"]]},{"id":"s79-backup-drs-c5","t":"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery","p":"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS) continuously replicates servers at block level into a low-cost staging area in AWS and launches recovery instances on demand.","k":[["Does DRS need a full duplicate fleet running?","No. Only low-cost staging resources run until recovery."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s79-backup-drs-c6","t":"Testing recovery","p":"A recovery plan is real only after it has been exercised with measured results.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s79-backup-drs-c7","t":"Backup monitoring and cost","p":"Monitor job success, alarm on failures and watch storage growth, because backup storage accumulates silently.","k":[["Compute the estimate for 5 TB, 2 percent daily change, 35 days.","5 + 0.02 x 5 x 35 = 8.5 TB."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s80-direct-connect-vpn","title":"Direct Connect and Site-to-Site VPN","c":[{"id":"s80-direct-connect-vpn-c1","t":"Site-to-Site VPN","p":"A Site-to-Site VPN creates two IPsec tunnels between your customer gateway device and AWS over the internet.","k":[["Why configure both tunnels?","AWS can take one down for maintenance; both give redundancy."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s80-direct-connect-vpn-c2","t":"Direct Connect basics","p":"AWS Direct Connect is a dedicated private network connection from your site to an AWS Direct Connect location.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s80-direct-connect-vpn-c3","t":"Virtual interfaces and the Direct Connect gateway","p":"A virtual interface (VIF) is a VLAN with a BGP session on a Direct Connect connection. A Direct Connect gateway lets one VIF reach VPCs in many Regions.","k":[["Which VIF type serves many VPCs through a transit gateway?","A transit VIF."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s80-direct-connect-vpn-c4","t":"Resilience models","p":"AWS recommends resilience levels from development up to maximum resiliency, based on the number of locations and links.","k":[],"o":[["Maximum","production that cannot lose connectivity","a lab environment"],["High","production with some tolerance for device failure","single device failure is unacceptable"],["Dev and test","non critical workloads","any production dependency"]]},{"id":"s80-direct-connect-vpn-c5","t":"BGP routing and path selection","p":"BGP exchanges routes between your router and AWS. When several paths exist, the most specific route and the documented preference order decide.","k":[["For 10.0.1.5, which wins, the /8 on DX or the /16 on VPN?","The /16 on VPN, because longest prefix wins."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s80-direct-connect-vpn-c6","t":"Hybrid DNS and monitoring","p":"Name resolution must work both ways, and the links must be monitored.","k":[["Which design is usual for production hybrid?","Direct Connect primary with VPN backup, or two resilient Direct Connect locations."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s81-transit-gateway","title":"Transit Gateway deep dive","c":[{"id":"s81-transit-gateway-c1","t":"Why a transit gateway","p":"A TGW is a hub. Each network attaches once, and the hub routes between them.","k":[["How many peerings does a full mesh of 10 VPCs need?","45."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s81-transit-gateway-c2","t":"Route tables, associations and propagation","p":"Each attachment is associated with one TGW route table that decides where its traffic goes. An attachment can propagate its routes into any number of tables.","k":[["How many route tables can one attachment be associated with?","One."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s81-transit-gateway-c3","t":"Segmentation patterns","p":"Use separate route tables to isolate environments while sharing common services.","k":[],"o":[["Separate tables","environments, compliance zones","a tiny network with one team"],["Single default table","a lab","any regulated workload"]]},{"id":"s81-transit-gateway-c4","t":"Centralised inspection and egress","p":"Send traffic through a central inspection VPC with firewalls, or through a central NAT, using appliance mode and route design.","k":[["Why enable appliance mode?","So both directions of a flow stay in the same AZ and a stateful appliance sees them."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s81-transit-gateway-c5","t":"Inter-Region peering and sharing","p":"TGW peering links gateways in different Regions. AWS RAM shares a gateway with other accounts.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s81-transit-gateway-c6","t":"Cost, limits and troubleshooting","p":"Costs have an hourly attachment fee and a per GB processing fee. Troubleshooting follows the route chain.","k":[["Why might traffic reach the destination but replies fail?","A missing return route in the destination VPC or TGW table."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s82-network-firewall-global-accelerator","title":"Network Firewall and Global Accelerator","c":[{"id":"s82-network-firewall-global-accelerator-c1","t":"What Network Firewall is","p":"AWS Network Firewall is a managed, stateful network firewall and intrusion prevention service deployed as endpoints in your VPC subnets.","k":[["Which service filters traffic by domain name inside a VPC?","Network Firewall (stateful domain list rules)."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s82-network-firewall-global-accelerator-c2","t":"Rule groups and domain filtering","p":"A rule group is a reusable set of rules. Stateful groups can allow or deny by domain list, or use Suricata rule strings.","k":[["Why size rule group capacity with headroom?","Capacity is fixed at creation and cannot be raised later."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s82-network-firewall-global-accelerator-c3","t":"Firewall deployment models","p":"Deploy firewall endpoints in each VPC (distributed) or in one inspection VPC reached through a transit gateway (centralised).","k":[],"o":[["Distributed","few VPCs with team-owned rules","many accounts needing one policy"],["Centralised","many VPCs and a security team","very high cross-AZ transit volume"],["Third party via GWLB","you must use a vendor firewall","a managed AWS option satisfies needs"]]},{"id":"s82-network-firewall-global-accelerator-c4","t":"Logging and tuning","p":"Send alert and flow logs somewhere searchable, then tune rules from real traffic.","k":[["Why start in alert mode?","To learn legitimate traffic before blocking it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s82-network-firewall-global-accelerator-c5","t":"Global Accelerator basics","p":"AWS Global Accelerator gives your application two static anycast IP addresses that route users onto the AWS backbone at the nearest edge location.","k":[["Why does failover not wait for DNS TTL?","Clients use fixed anycast IPs and Global Accelerator itself reroutes."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s82-network-firewall-global-accelerator-c6","t":"Global Accelerator versus CloudFront and Route 53","p":"CloudFront caches HTTP content at the edge. Route 53 answers DNS. Global Accelerator speeds up and stabilises any TCP or UDP path.","k":[],"o":[["CloudFront","content is cacheable HTTP","you need fixed IPs for a TCP service"],["Global Accelerator","static IP or non-HTTP traffic","caching would solve the problem"],["Route 53 only","simple multi-Region failover with tolerable TTL","seconds-level failover is required"]]},{"id":"s82-network-firewall-global-accelerator-c7","t":"Multi-Region failover with Global Accelerator","p":"Place endpoint groups in two Regions and use traffic dials and health checks to steer traffic.","k":[["How do you drain a Region for maintenance?","Set its endpoint group traffic dial to 0."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s83-cloudfront-deep-dive","title":"CloudFront deep dive","c":[{"id":"s83-cloudfront-deep-dive-c1","t":"Distributions, origins and behaviors","p":"A distribution has one or more origins (S3, ALB, custom HTTP) and behaviors that map URL path patterns to an origin and cache settings.","k":[["In which Region must a CloudFront ACM certificate live?","us-east-1."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s83-cloudfront-deep-dive-c2","t":"Cache keys, TTLs and invalidation","p":"The cache key decides which requests share one cached object. TTLs decide how long it lives.","k":[["Better than invalidating on every deploy?","Content-hashed file names with long TTLs."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s83-cloudfront-deep-dive-c3","t":"Securing origins","p":"Origin Access Control (OAC) lets only CloudFront read a private S3 bucket. For ALB origins, restrict by secret header or managed prefix list.","k":[["How does an S3 policy bind access to one distribution?","A condition on AWS:SourceArn."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s83-cloudfront-deep-dive-c4","t":"Signed URLs, cookies and WAF","p":"Signed URLs and cookies restrict who may fetch content. AWS WAF filters malicious requests at the edge.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s83-cloudfront-deep-dive-c5","t":"Edge compute with Functions and Lambda@Edge","p":"Run small code at the edge. CloudFront Functions are tiny JavaScript for viewer events. Lambda@Edge is full Lambda for viewer and origin events.","k":[["Which option can call an external API?","Lambda@Edge, not CloudFront Functions."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s83-cloudfront-deep-dive-c6","t":"Origin failover and performance","p":"Origin groups fail over to a secondary origin on selected status codes. Compression, HTTP/3 and Origin Shield improve performance.","k":[["What header shows a cache hit?","X-Cache: Hit from cloudfront."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s83-cloudfront-deep-dive-c7","t":"Logging, cost and troubleshooting","p":"Standard logs, real-time logs and CloudWatch metrics show hit ratio, errors and bytes.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s84-organizations-control-tower-migration","title":"Migrating an Organization to a Control Tower landing zone","c":[{"id":"s84-organizations-control-tower-migration-c1","t":"What Control Tower adds","p":"AWS Control Tower sets up a multi-account landing zone with a log archive account, an audit account, IAM Identity Center, baseline CloudTrail and Config, and controls (guardrails).","k":[["Which two accounts does Control Tower create for logging and audit?","Log Archive and Audit."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s84-organizations-control-tower-migration-c2","t":"Pre-migration assessment","p":"Inventory what already exists so Control Tower does not collide with your current setup.","k":[["Why choose the home Region carefully?","It is hard to change later and hosts Control Tower resources."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s84-organizations-control-tower-migration-c3","t":"Enabling the landing zone","p":"Set up Control Tower from the management account, creating or reusing the Security OU accounts.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s84-organizations-control-tower-migration-c4","t":"Enrolling existing accounts and OUs","p":"Enrolment installs the Control Tower baseline in an existing account so it follows the same guardrails as new ones.","k":[["Which role must exist in an account before it can be enrolled?","AWSControlTowerExecution."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s84-organizations-control-tower-migration-c5","t":"Controls and SCP migration","p":"Replace homemade SCPs with Control Tower controls where an equivalent exists, and keep custom SCPs for the rest.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s84-organizations-control-tower-migration-c6","t":"Customisation with AFT and CfCT","p":"Account Factory for Terraform (AFT) and Customizations for Control Tower (CfCT) add your own baselines, networking and roles to new accounts.","k":[["What triggers AFT to build an account?","A merged account request file in Git."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s84-organizations-control-tower-migration-c7","t":"Rollback, drift and operations","p":"Plan how to repair drift and what to do if enrolment fails.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s85-service-catalog","title":"AWS Service Catalog","c":[{"id":"s85-service-catalog-c1","t":"Portfolios and products","p":"A product is an approved template (for example a standard S3 bucket or a three-tier stack). A portfolio groups products and decides who can launch them.","k":[["What is a provisioned product?","The running instance created when a user launches a product."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s85-service-catalog-c2","t":"Launch constraints and least privilege","p":"A launch constraint names an IAM role that Service Catalog assumes to create the resources, so the end user needs no permissions beyond launching.","k":[["Why does a launch constraint help least privilege?","The user needs no direct create permissions. The service assumes a scoped role."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s85-service-catalog-c3","t":"Template constraints and parameters","p":"Template constraints restrict the parameter values users may pick, for example instance sizes or Regions.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s85-service-catalog-c4","t":"Sharing across accounts","p":"Portfolios can be shared with accounts or an entire Organization so every account sees the same catalog.","k":[["What must exist in each recipient account for a shared launch role?","A role with the same name."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s85-service-catalog-c5","t":"Versions, updates and lifecycle","p":"Each product has numbered versions. Users update provisioned products to a newer version through a change set.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s85-service-catalog-c6","t":"Service Catalog versus other approaches","p":"Service Catalog sits between a free-for-all and fully central ticket-based provisioning.","k":[["When prefer Service Catalog?","When you want guarded self-service launches with launch roles and portfolios across accounts."]],"o":[["Service Catalog","non-engineers or many accounts need guarded launches","a pure Git workflow already works"],["Module registry with pipeline","engineers own infrastructure in Git","users lack Git or CI access"],["Internal portal over both","you want one front door","no platform team exists"]]},{"id":"s85-service-catalog-c7","t":"AppRegistry and governance","p":"AWS Service Catalog AppRegistry groups resources into applications with metadata, so cost, compliance and ownership roll up to an application.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s86-resilience-hub-fis","title":"Resilience Hub and Fault Injection Service","c":[{"id":"s86-resilience-hub-fis-c1","t":"Resilience Hub assessments","p":"AWS Resilience Hub analyses an application's resources against resilience policies you set and reports gaps with recommendations.","k":[["Name the four disruption types in a policy.","Application (software), infrastructure (hardware), Availability Zone and Region."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s86-resilience-hub-fis-c2","t":"Operational recommendations","p":"Resilience Hub produces more than design advice. It suggests alarms, standard operating procedures and tests.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s86-resilience-hub-fis-c3","t":"FIS experiment templates","p":"AWS Fault Injection Service runs fault actions on your resources according to an experiment template.","k":[["What aborts an experiment automatically?","A stop condition, usually a CloudWatch alarm."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s86-resilience-hub-fis-c4","t":"Stop conditions and safety","p":"Experiments must be bounded. Stop conditions, narrow targets and a dedicated role keep them safe.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s86-resilience-hub-fis-c5","t":"AZ failure and database failover tests","p":"Two high-value tests simulate losing an AZ and forcing a database failover.","k":[["Which hidden single points of failure do AZ tests often reveal?","A single NAT gateway, hard coded AZs, or single-AZ dependencies."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s86-resilience-hub-fis-c6","t":"Game days and continuous validation","p":"A game day is a planned exercise where a team runs failures and practises response. Continuous experiments keep results fresh.","k":[["What makes a game day useful?","A clear hypothesis, bounded failure, measurement and follow-up fixes."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s87-trusted-advisor-compute-optimizer","title":"Trusted Advisor and Compute Optimizer","c":[{"id":"s87-trusted-advisor-compute-optimizer-c1","t":"What Trusted Advisor checks","p":"Trusted Advisor inspects your account against best practice checks in categories: cost optimization, performance, security, fault tolerance, service limits and operational excellence.","k":[["Which Trusted Advisor check category warns before you hit quotas?","Service limits."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s87-trusted-advisor-compute-optimizer-c2","t":"Automating Trusted Advisor","p":"Trusted Advisor publishes status changes to EventBridge, so findings can raise tickets or trigger remediation.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s87-trusted-advisor-compute-optimizer-c3","t":"How Compute Optimizer works","p":"Compute Optimizer analyses CloudWatch utilisation history with machine learning and recommends better configurations.","k":[["Which metric needs the CloudWatch agent for EC2 recommendations?","Memory utilisation."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s87-trusted-advisor-compute-optimizer-c4","t":"Acting on recommendations safely","p":"A recommendation is a hypothesis. Test it on a copy or in a canary before you change production.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s87-trusted-advisor-compute-optimizer-c5","t":"Cost Optimization Hub and Savings Plans","p":"Cost Optimization Hub consolidates savings opportunities from many sources, including Compute Optimizer, idle resources and commitment recommendations.","k":[["Why rightsize before buying Savings Plans?","A commitment sized to wasteful usage locks in the waste."]],"o":[["Delete idle first","resources have no traffic","they are quiet DR standbys"],["Right-size second","utilisation is consistently low","spiky workloads need headroom"],["Commit last","the baseline is stable","architecture changes are planned soon"]]},{"id":"s87-trusted-advisor-compute-optimizer-c6","t":"Governance and reporting","p":"Put recommendations into a regular review with owners, so improvements actually happen.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s88-config-conformance-packs","title":"AWS Config rules and conformance packs","c":[{"id":"s88-config-conformance-packs-c1","t":"Config recorder, rules and evaluations","p":"The configuration recorder stores a history of resource configuration items. Rules evaluate those items and mark each resource compliant or non-compliant.","k":[["Which two triggers can a Config rule use?","Configuration change and periodic."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s88-config-conformance-packs-c2","t":"Custom rules with Lambda and Guard","p":"When no managed rule fits, write a custom Lambda rule or a Guard policy-as-code rule.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s88-config-conformance-packs-c3","t":"Conformance packs","p":"A conformance pack is a bundle of Config rules and remediation actions deployed together as a single template.","k":[["Where do you deploy an organization conformance pack from?","The management account or a delegated administrator."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s88-config-conformance-packs-c4","t":"Aggregators and advanced queries","p":"An aggregator collects Config data from many accounts and Regions into one view. Advanced queries use SQL-like syntax over resource configuration.","k":[["What does an aggregator give a central team?","A single place to see configuration and compliance across accounts and Regions."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s88-config-conformance-packs-c5","t":"Automatic remediation","p":"A Config rule can trigger an SSM Automation runbook to fix a non-compliant resource, automatically or after approval.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s88-config-conformance-packs-c6","t":"Config versus Control Tower, Security Hub and cost","p":"Config is the detective data layer. Control Tower, Security Hub and others consume it.","k":[["Why trim recorded resource types?","To reduce cost by skipping resources and churn you never evaluate."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s89-vpc-lattice","title":"Amazon VPC Lattice","c":[{"id":"s89-vpc-lattice-c1","t":"Why Lattice exists","p":"Lattice lets one service call another by name, across VPCs and accounts, with routing and access control handled by AWS.","k":[["Can two VPCs with overlapping CIDRs use Lattice?","Yes, since clients connect through Lattice rather than routing to target IPs."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s89-vpc-lattice-c2","t":"Services, listeners, rules and target groups","p":"A service has listeners (protocol and port). Rules match requests and forward to target groups, like a managed ALB for service to service traffic.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s89-vpc-lattice-c3","t":"Service networks and associations","p":"A service network is a logical boundary. Services and VPCs are associated with it, and associated VPCs can reach the associated services.","k":[["How are service networks shared between accounts?","With AWS RAM."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s89-vpc-lattice-c4","t":"Auth policies and SigV4","p":"Lattice evaluates auth policies (resource policies in IAM policy language) on service networks and services, for clients that sign requests with SigV4.","k":[["Which auth type requires signed requests?","AWS_IAM."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s89-vpc-lattice-c5","t":"Lattice with Kubernetes","p":"The AWS Gateway API Controller maps Kubernetes Gateway API objects (Gateway, HTTPRoute) to Lattice services and target groups.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s89-vpc-lattice-c6","t":"Lattice versus PrivateLink, Transit Gateway and meshes","p":"These tools overlap but solve different problems.","k":[["Which supports overlapping CIDRs and path routing?","VPC Lattice."]],"o":[["VPC Lattice","many services across VPCs and accounts need routing and IAM auth","you only need raw network reachability"],["PrivateLink","one service shared with consumers who may overlap","you need path routing"],["Transit Gateway","broad network connectivity including on-premises","you want per-service authorisation"]]},{"id":"s89-vpc-lattice-c7","t":"Observability and limits","p":"Lattice emits access logs and metrics for each service, giving visibility into service to service traffic.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s90-bedrock-sagemaker-platform","title":"Amazon Bedrock and SageMaker for platform teams","c":[{"id":"s90-bedrock-sagemaker-platform-c1","t":"Bedrock and SageMaker compared","p":"Amazon Bedrock gives API access to foundation models from several providers. Amazon SageMaker is a platform to build, train, tune and host your own models.","k":[["Which service bills per token?","Bedrock on-demand inference."]],"o":[["Bedrock","you need text, image or embedding models behind an API","you must control model weights and hosting"],["SageMaker","you train or fine-tune custom models","a managed model API already fits"],["Both","custom model plus a general assistant","a single model serves the use case"]]},{"id":"s90-bedrock-sagemaker-platform-c2","t":"Access control for Bedrock","p":"Bedrock access is controlled by IAM. Model access must also be enabled, and IAM policies restrict which models each role may invoke.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s90-bedrock-sagemaker-platform-c3","t":"Guardrails, RAG and agents","p":"Guardrails filter inputs and outputs. Knowledge Bases add retrieval from your documents (RAG). Agents let models call tools.","k":[["Why treat model output as untrusted?","It can be wrong or manipulated, so validate it before using it in other systems."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s90-bedrock-sagemaker-platform-c4","t":"SageMaker building blocks","p":"SageMaker offers managed notebooks, training jobs, pipelines, a model registry and inference endpoints.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s90-bedrock-sagemaker-platform-c5","t":"Network and data security","p":"Keep training data and endpoints private, encrypted and auditable.","k":[["How do workloads reach Bedrock without the internet?","Through an interface VPC endpoint for bedrock-runtime."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s90-bedrock-sagemaker-platform-c6","t":"Cost and quota management","p":"AI costs scale with tokens or instance hours. Quotas limit throughput.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s90-bedrock-sagemaker-platform-c7","t":"Operating AI workloads","p":"Operate AI services like any production service, with extra attention to evaluation, drift and safety.","k":[["Compute the per-request cost above for 100,000 requests.","0.0135 x 100,000 = 1,350 dollars at the assumed prices."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s91-which-service-for-which-job","title":"Which AWS service for which job","c":[{"id":"s91-which-service-for-which-job-c1","t":"A decision method","p":"Ask the same questions in the same order: what is the workload shape, what are the constraints, and what is the cheapest thing that meets them.","k":[["What should a decision record include besides the choice?","Context, rejected options and the trigger to revisit."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s91-which-service-for-which-job-c2","t":"Choosing compute","p":"Compute ranges from functions to full servers. Move toward more control only when needed.","k":[["Which compute option supports tasks longer than 15 minutes without servers?","Fargate (ECS or Batch), not Lambda."]],"o":[["Lambda","event driven, spiky, short tasks","steady high load or long running"],["Fargate","containers with a small team","GPUs or privileged mode"],["EKS","Kubernetes tooling is a requirement","no one can run upgrades"],["EC2","special hardware or licences","you want no patching"]]},{"id":"s91-which-service-for-which-job-c3","t":"Choosing data stores","p":"Pick the data store by access pattern first, then scale and consistency.","k":[],"o":[["DynamoDB","known access patterns and huge scale","ad hoc queries and joins"],["Aurora","relational integrity and moderate scale","millions of writes per second on a key"],["S3 plus Athena","infrequent analytical queries","low-latency transactions"]]},{"id":"s91-which-service-for-which-job-c4","t":"Choosing messaging and integration","p":"Use queues for work, topics for fan-out, event buses for routing, streams for replay, and workflows for orchestration.","k":[["Which service orchestrates multi-step workflows with retries and approvals?","Step Functions."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s91-which-service-for-which-job-c5","t":"Choosing networking and edge","p":"Pick network services by scope: inside a VPC, between VPCs, to on-premises, and to the internet.","k":[],"o":[["Peering","two VPCs, simple","many VPCs"],["Transit Gateway","many VPCs and on-premises","per-service authorisation"],["Lattice","app-level routing and IAM auth","raw network transit"]]},{"id":"s91-which-service-for-which-job-c6","t":"Choosing security and governance services","p":"Layer prevention, detection and response: SCPs and IAM prevent, Config and GuardDuty detect, Security Hub and automation respond.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s91-which-service-for-which-job-c7","t":"Choosing observability and operations","p":"Start with the managed tooling, then add third-party tooling where it fills a gap.","k":[["Which services provide traces on AWS?","X-Ray, or OpenTelemetry through a collector."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s92-architecture-scenarios-part1","title":"Architecture scenarios part one","c":[{"id":"s92-architecture-scenarios-part1-c1","t":"How to read the scenarios","p":"Each scenario starts with requirements, then picks services by the rules from the previous section, then checks failure and cost.","k":[["200 requests per second at 250 ms average needs how much concurrency?","200 x 0.25 = 50."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s92-architecture-scenarios-part1-c2","t":"Scenario 1 Static website","p":"A marketing site with global visitors, a few deployments per day and no server logic.","k":[["Why give index.html a different cache header from hashed assets?","It must always revalidate, while hashed assets never change."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s92-architecture-scenarios-part1-c3","t":"Scenario 2 Three-tier web application","p":"A customer-facing web app: presentation, application and database tiers with 99.9 percent availability.","k":[["Why add one task beyond the computed count?","To keep capacity if one AZ is lost."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s92-architecture-scenarios-part1-c4","t":"Scenario 3 Event-driven pipeline","p":"Uploaded files trigger validation, enrichment and storage, with bursts of 10,000 files in minutes.","k":[["What protects the database from the burst?","Reserved concurrency on the function plus the queue buffer."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s92-architecture-scenarios-part1-c5","t":"Scenario 4 Batch processing","p":"A nightly job processes 2 TB of data in under 3 hours using many short tasks.","k":[["How many vCPU-hours does the job use?","200 chunks x 0.5 hour = 100 vCPU-hours."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s92-architecture-scenarios-part1-c6","t":"Failure review and cost drivers","p":"After the design, ask what breaks first, how you know, and what each costs.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s93-architecture-scenarios-part2","title":"Architecture scenarios part two","c":[{"id":"s93-architecture-scenarios-part2-c1","t":"Scenario 5 Data lake","p":"Collect application logs, database exports and clickstream for analytics by many teams with access control.","k":[["What gives column level access control on the lake?","Lake Formation grants and LF-tags."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s93-architecture-scenarios-part2-c2","t":"Scenario 6 Multi-Region active-active","p":"A global service must keep running if a whole Region fails and serve users from the nearest Region.","k":[["What is the main data risk with multi-writer replication?","Conflicting concurrent writes to the same item; last writer wins unless you design around it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s93-architecture-scenarios-part2-c3","t":"Scenario 7 SaaS multi-tenant","p":"A SaaS product serves hundreds of customers from shared infrastructure with strict data isolation and per-tenant billing.","k":[["What stops a tenant reading another tenant's items in DynamoDB?","An IAM LeadingKeys condition bound to the tenant identity, plus tenant checks in code."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s93-architecture-scenarios-part2-c4","t":"Scenario 8 Hybrid with on-premises","p":"Core databases stay in a data centre for now while new services run in AWS and must call them with low latency.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s93-architecture-scenarios-part2-c5","t":"Choosing between the eight designs","p":"Real systems combine patterns. Use the same questions to select which parts you need.","k":[["When is multi-Region justified?","When a measured business need exceeds what Multi-AZ can deliver."]],"o":[["Multi-Region active-active","RTO of seconds across a Region failure is required","Multi-AZ already meets the SLO"],["Silo tenancy","contracts or regulation require isolation","tenants are small and numerous"],["Direct Connect","steady high bandwidth or strict latency","occasional small transfers"]]},{"id":"s93-architecture-scenarios-part2-c6","t":"Failure review and decision records","p":"End every design with a failure review and a written decision record.","k":[],"o":[]}]}]};